\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korea won falls briefly on suspected  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoo Choonsik  \par \uc2 497 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2004\par 07:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, Nov 22 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's won \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped suddenly from 7-year highs in Monday afternoon trade on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  just hours after the finance minister held a rare meeting to discuss its rise with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The won             fell to 1,068.8 per dollar in local trade soon\par after 0500 GMT, having earlier reached 1,060.0, its strongest against\par the dollar since November 1997. It stayed only briefly at its low,\par which was just weaker than Friday's close of 1,068.7, before recovering\par towards 1,065.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 "The dollar rose suddenly because of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by authorities, but there are still lots of dollars in the market," said a dealer at a local bank.  \par \par \uc2 The strength of the won, which has gained 6 percent this month against the dollar and 12 percent so far this year, also sent the local stock market tumbling almost 2 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier on Monday, Finance Minister Lee Hun-jai asked \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Park Seung for a stronger stance in capping the won's rise, according to a government source.  \par \par \uc2 "He asked Governor Park that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  take bolder and more aggressive counter measures," the source said by telephone, adding that this could include the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  using its right to print money.  \par \par \uc2 However, dealers say any impact of such moves would only be limited unless they are followed by actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "This is driven by a dollar oversupply and looks inevitable," said Park Yong-il, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at DBS Bank's branch in Seoul. "Many appear doubtful that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can keep printing money without limit to absorb all the supply."  \par \par \uc2 The rare meeting between Lee and the governor of the Bank of Korea came as the won's rally raised concerns about the export-dependent economy being damaged as slowing exports combine with a persistent slump in local demand.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea has taken the leading role in controlling the won's rise since the finance ministry was criticised last month in parliament for keeping the won undervalued.  \par \par \uc2 The amount of money the ministry can mobilise to fund \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also been limited by parliament.  \par \par \uc2 The rising won has added to concerns about Chinese demand for exports after Beijing raised interest rates for the first time in nine years to step up a drive to cool its economy.  \par \par \uc2 China is the largest market for South Korean exports, soaking up a fifth of its total shipments abroad.  \par \par \uc2 Last week the finance minister all but abandoned a long-standing target of 5 percent economic growth this year, saying the chance of meeting the forecast was extremely slim.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The firmer won also hit export-reliant manufacturers on the Seoul stock\par market. Chipmaker Samsung Electronics Co. Ltd.  fell 3.1 percent and\par automaker Hyundai Motor Co.             lost 2.4 percent. (Additional\par reporting by Lee Shin-hyung and Cheon Jong-woo).\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041122e0bm0051g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\