\par\par \b \uc2 Seoul shares to open lower, oil price dip to support.   \b0\par\par\uc2 450 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 2004\par 01:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, Nov 17 (Reuters) - Seoul stocks look set to open lower on Wednesday, pausing from a recent rally spurred by an unexpected cut in interest rates, but falling oil prices may boost export and utilities stocks.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments that the won's recent gains had been too rapid may also help exporters such as Hyundai Motor Co. .   \par \par \uc2 "The domestic markets are likely to continue a correction after recent gains, following a similar movement among U.S. shares," said Kim Hak-kyun, an analyst at Goodmorning Shinhan Securities. "But there is still potential for further gains after the rate cut and as oil prices continue falling."   \par \par \uc2 The benchmark Korea Composite Stock Price Index (KOSPI) fell 0.65 percent on Tuesday to 876.61 points.   \par \par \uc2 Seoul stock trading is to start at 0100 GMT, an hour later than usual, because of the country-wide college entrance examinations. The market will close an hour later than usual.   \par \par \uc2 U.S. shares fell on Tuesday, marking a pause for breath after the market's strong post-election rally.   \par \par \uc2 U.S. crude oil futures tumbled, easing concerns about the impact of higher energy costs on corporate profits. Oil traded at $46.16 a barrel on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 South Korea is the world's fourth-largest importer.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A senior South Korean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official said the won         was\par rising too fast.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The warning followed data showing a jump in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves amid suspected heavy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to control a rise in the won.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS TO WATCH   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - SK Networks Co. Ltd.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The trading arm of SK Group said on Tuesday its shareholders had agreed to a capital reduction and share split as part of a restructuring plan.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Hynix Semiconductor Inc.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The chip maker said on Tuesday it would form a joint venture with a European chip maker to invest $375 million in a factory in China.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But U.S. technology developer Rambus (RMBS.O) said on Tuesday a judge has issued a favourable ruling in a legal row with Hynix.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Samsung Electronics Co. Ltd.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. semiconductor packaging technology company Tessera Technologies Inc. said on Tuesday the South Korean chip maker had agreed to make royalty payments to settle their legal dispute.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PREVIOUS CLOSE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The December KOSPI 200 futures index  shed 0.45 point on Tuesday to\par 113.65 and the underlying KOSPI 200 spot index          lost 0.79 point\par to 113.30.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The over-the-counter Kosdaq lost 0.54 percent to finish at 370.93.    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Dow Jones industrial average  fell 0.59 percent to 10,487.65, and\par the tech-laced Nasdaq composite         slipped 0.74 percent to end at\par 2,078.62.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041117e0bh00108\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
