\par\par \b \uc2 Bk of Korea: Worried  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Sentiment 'Excessively' Lopsided  \b0\par\par\uc2 459 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2004\par 00:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--In the first verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from local authorities in many months, the Bank of Korea Wednesday said the U.S. dollar's fall against the won has been of a greater magnitude compared with the trend in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and warned it is "fully prepared to take necessary action."  \par \par \uc2 "We're worried that the sentiment in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is excessively tilted to one direction," Rhee Gwang-Ju, director-general of the bank's international department, said in a statement.  \par \par \uc2 In an apparent warning to exporters who lately have been blamed for the sharp dollar retreat, the Bank of Korea said it is "closely watching" the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  activities of companies as well as individual onshore and offshore players.  \par \par \uc2 The unusually strong-worded statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes after the dollar Tuesday dipped to levels not seen since 1997, at the height of the Asian financial crisis. [ 10-11-04 0025GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 The statement Wednesday also marks the first time the Bank of Korea has stepped past the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy to helm \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies since the second half of 2003. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been deferring to the ministry, which has publicly favored a weak won policy to support the export-reliant domestic economy.  \par \par \uc2 The finance ministry has kept a conspicuously low profile on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues recently, after being grounded by politicians for its earlier, all-out won-weakening efforts. Imported inflationary pressure due to strong global oil prices and stubbornly sluggish domestic consumption have also weakened the ministry's opposition to a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Tuesday, the dollar finished at KRW1,103.6, down from KRW1,105.3 Monday. Tuesday's level was the lowest close since KRW1,085 on Nov. 24, 1997.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has been declining against \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the globe due to mounting concerns about the U.S. deficit, but its fall against the won stands out.  \par \par \uc2 The won has gained 7.5% against the dollar this year, compared with a 1.7% rise in the Japanese yen and a 3.2% advance in the New Taiwan dollar. This means products from these competing exporters are likely to be priced more attractively than South Korean goods to consumers in key markets like the U.S.  \par \par \uc2 Many economists have warned the won is gaining too fast, considering the deteriorating fundamentals of \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy.  \par \par \uc2 Wednesday's verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far has had sufficient effect in tempering the won-bullish sentiment, sending the dollar as high as KRW1,111 since market opening.  \par \par \uc2 At 0100 GMT, the dollar was at 1,107.6.  \par \par \uc2 -By Hye-Seung Seo, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165; hyeseung.seo@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Lena Lee [ 10-11-04 0111GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041109e0b9001ep\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
