\par\par \b \uc2 S Korea Govt: Monitoring USD/KRW,No Official Comment Yet  \b0\par\par\uc2 407 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2004\par 02:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--South Korea's government is "closely" monitoring the won's movement against the U.S. dollar to determine whether it needs to take action after the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a four-year high Monday, a senior official said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0030 GMT, the dollar plunged to 1,136.3 won, down from the KRW1,140.7 close Friday. It marked the U.S. unit's lowest level since Nov. 15, 2000, when it ended at KRW1,135.8. It is also the first time the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slid below KRW1,140 in four years.  \par \par \uc2 The won's gains came after the U.S. dollar slid below 107 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "We're watching the market and whether various factors require our actions," Kim Ik-Joo, director of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy bureau, said.  \par \par \uc2 The government hasn't decided to issue an official oral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet, he said. The won, he told Dow Jones Newswires, was reacting "too sensitively" to the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's movement. [ 25-10-04 0052GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Kim said the government is studying whether the won's rise reflects differences in economic fundamentals between South Korea and Japan. While Korea's economy is yet to recover from a slowdown, the local government believes the Japanese economy is performing better than expected.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese government has kept a low profile on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters in recent months. \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 , too, has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  less in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the U.S. dollar amid rising global oil prices that have raised concerns over imported inflation. The country is the world's fourth-largest crude importer.  \par \par \uc2 Since September last year, Korean authorities have publicly favored a weaker won to sustain an export-reliant economic recovery amid sluggish domestic demand. The government spent heavily until the first half of this year to curb the won's gains.  \par \par \uc2 Still, as of Monday, the won had risen 4.7% since the beginning of the year.  \par \par \uc2 The government has issued KRW1 trillion in treasurys so far this month to fund its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It issued KRW2 trillion in treasurys each month from July to September.  \par \par \uc2 "We can still issue additional treasurys for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilization this month to send a signal to the market," Kim said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Hye-Seung Seo, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165; hyeseung.seo@dowjones.com [ 25-10-04 0135GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041025e0ap0000a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
