\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-S.Korea sees consumer sentiment improving further.  \b0\par\par\uc2 446 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2004\par 05:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, Oct 15 (Reuters) - South Korea expects consumer sentiment to see a further improvement in October after a recent survey showed sentiment brightening for the first time in five months in September, Finance Minister Lee Hun-jai said on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Lee also said during a weekly media briefing the economy, the third largest in Asia, should achieve 5 percent growth in 2005 helped by government steps to boost construction spending.  \par \par \uc2 "The government expects further improvement in consumer sentiment in October," Lee said. The key consumer expectation index rose slightly in September, although still showed a majority of consumers remained pessimistic about the economy.  \par \par \uc2 "Plans to expand fiscal spending and boost construction demand will enable the country to achieve optimal growth potential," Lee said. The government has said South Korea's economy has the potential to grow by 5 percent or more annually.  \par \par \uc2 The ruling party and the government recently announced a string of measures to boost the economy, including income tax cuts and increased budget spending on projects to create more jobs and boost investment. The steps are pending parliamentary approval.  \par \par \uc2 The government is concerned economic growth next year will fall below its current target of 5 percent mainly because net exports - income from exports minus expenditure on imports - will stagnate after a sharp rise in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Instead, the government is pining its hopes on the private consumption and the construction industry, together accounting for about 60 percent of the country's annual gross domestic product, leading economic growth in 2005.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data shows net exports were responsible for nearly three-quarters of the 5.5 percent growth in gross domestic product in the year through the second quarter.  \par \par \uc2 Despite inflation worries, a delayed recovery in domestic demand has forced the Bank of Korea into cutting its overnight call rate target by a combined 75 basis points to a record 3.5 percent since early last year.  \par \par \uc2 But record high oil prices helped prevent the bank from delivering a fourth cut last week, defying market expectations that rates would be cut to 3.25 percent.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, Lee repeated the government's official stance that it was\par not targetting won/dollar             at a specific level. But he also\par said the government needed to have the capacity to deter any\par speculative forces aiming to send the won up or down sharply.\par   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has heavily \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , notably in late 2003, in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to keep dollar inflows from exports and share buying by foreign investors from pushing the won higher, saying an appreciation in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would hurt its exports when domestic demand remained in a slump.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041015e0af002jr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
