\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-S.Korea fin min says won not too cheap.   \b0\par\par\uc2 594 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2004\par 09:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SEOUL, Oct 11 (Reuters) - South Korea's finance minister dismissed\par criticism on Monday that the government's heavy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cap the\par won's         rise versus the dollar had done the economy more harm\par than good.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Minister Lee Hun-jai told a parliamentary committee audit on economic policy that the won had actually strengthened more than the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of smaller regional rivals Taiwan, Thailand and Singapore since 2002. He did not comment on a more recent timeframe.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 "The country has not been keeping the exchange rate high on purpose," Lee said.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said in a separate report to parliament that the won's value against the U.S. dollar was not far from an appropriate level when taking into account the country's economic health, but it did not mention a specific rate.   \par \par \uc2 "The won has appreciated 14.4 percent on average since 2002, compared with a 3.5 percent gain in Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , 6.8 percent in Thailand's and 9.8 percent in Singapore's," Lee said, referring to the value of each \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. unit.   \par \par \uc2 South Korea's parliament, in which the ruling Uri Party holds a slim majority, is undergoing an annual audit of state affairs.   \par \par \uc2 Earlier on Monday, lawmaker Lee Hye-hoon of the opposition Grand National Party said government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the won's rise has contributed to discouraging investment and private consumption.   \par \par \uc2 She also slammed in a statement the cost of the policy and said the government had to pay nearly 5 trillion won ($4.35 billion) in interest on bonds issued to fund its dollar purchases in the first eight months of 2004.   \par \par \uc2 "Since the 1990s, the (won/dollar) exchange rate has had a negative relation with growth in domestic demand and with growth in capital investment," she said, adding that a cheaper won had made imported materials more expensive and kept inflation high.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  has heavily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the won from rising due to dollar inflows from strong exports and foreign stock investments since late last year, leaving the won 0.5 percent weaker than a year before at the end of 2003.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par This was in stark contrast to more than 10 percent gains in the\par Japanese yen  and the euro        respectively against the U.S. dollar\par over the same period.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves rose about a quarter to $174.45 billion, the world's fourth-largest, in the year through the end of September. Dealers say much of that growth represented direct dollar purchases from the market.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , however, repeated the government's stance that the current exchange rate was not a poor reflection of the country's economic fundamentals.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The criticism came a few weeks after one of the seven policy board members of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Bank of Korea, told reporters \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  did little to help boost the economy and harmed consumption and consumer price stability.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finance ministry officials have said South Korea could not allow the won to appreciate significantly at a time when the economy, the third-largest in Asia, is relying heavily on exports for its recovery since hitting a brief recession in 2003.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government expects gross domestic product in 2004 to be at least 5 percent higher than a year ago, but major research institutions have recently downgraded their forecasts to far less than that, citing stubbornly weak local demand. ($1=1150.0 Won) (Additional reporting by Lim Moon-kyung).    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041011e0ab005n8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
