\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korean official-Little won impact seen with yuan float.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gertrude Chavez   \par \uc2 445 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2004\par 01:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Oct 2 (Reuters) - The South Korean won is not expected to strengthen sharply against the dollar should the Chinese government decide to remove the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg against the greenback, a top Korean finance official told Reuters on Saturday.   \par \par \uc2 The Chinese yuan, which is effectively pegged at 8.28 to the dollar, has been one of the major topics all weekend at the meeting of the Group of Seven rich economies and the semi-annual gathering of the International Monetary Fund and World Bank.   \par \par \uc2 China on Friday quashed expectations it would move to a more flexible exchange rate regime anytime soon.   \par \par \uc2 But if China decides to remove the peg, that could have a widespread impact on other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  because they all compete in the global marketplace.   \par \par \uc2 Not so for South Korea, said Joong-Kyung Choi, Director-General of the Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy's international finance bureau.   \par \par \uc2 "The yuan float will have limited impact on the won because our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has appreciated," considerably over the past year, Choi said. The won has closely tracked the yen because of the similarities in the Japanese and South Korean economies.   \par \par \uc2 The won has been one of the strongest Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar, Choi said, speaking on the sidelines of the semi-annual meetings of the International Monetary Fund and World Bank.   \par \par \uc2 Since August this year, the won has risen around 1.5 percent against the dollar thanks to a still sizable trade surplus, estimated at $1.8 billion in August.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts say South Korea, just like Japan, would have liked a much weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it makes that country's exports cheaper overseas. For this reason, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sometimes relied on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or buying dollars and selling the won, to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak and preserve that competitive price advantage, analysts noted.   \par \par \uc2 But more than the prospect of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts fretted about South Korea's economic prospects.   \par \par \uc2 The combination of relatively high unemployment - a three-year high of 3.6 percent in August - and the slow pace of job creation will continue to weigh on consumer confidence and salaries into 2005. That could threaten to limit the scope for an upturn in consumer spending in the months ahead.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts at Scotia Capital in Toronto, however, said in a research note that a gradual recovery in domestic spending in 2005 will reinvigorate investor confidence in Korean assets.   \par \par \uc2 "This, coupled with the appreciation of the yen against the dollar will induce the Korean authorities to accept some companion strengthening of the won against the dollar," said Scotia Capital.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041002e0a200gl9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
