\par\par \b \uc2 S Korea Govt Official:Strong  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Possible 2H  \b0\par\par\uc2 356 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2004\par 03:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--Buoyed by expected parliamentary approval of a sharp increase in this year's treasury issues for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy toned up its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  voice Thursday, with a senior official warning that its market actions to weaken the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will come in the form of a "downpour" from now on.  \par \par \uc2 "From now on, we will show the art of concentration. When we take part in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, it will be a downpour and not a drizzle to make our intentions clear," Choi Joong-Kyung, director-general of the ministry's international bureau, told Dow Jones Newswires and Yonhap Infomax.  \par \par \uc2 His comment comes as the Parliament is expected to give a go-ahead Thursday afternoon to the ministry's proposal to hike this year's ceiling to issue treasurys to fund its campaign to keep the won stable against the dollar by another 11 trillion won ($=KRW1,156) on top of an initial KRW7.8 trillion. [ 15-07-04 0152GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Choi explained that his ministry concluded that it would be appropriate to have KRW11 trillion more in coffers for its second-half \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy based on domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market size, dollar/yen movement and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds of competing Asian countries.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar/won jumped immediately after Choi's "downpour" comment, with the dollar trading at KRW1,157.7 - more than a three-week high - at 0210 GMT versus its opening at KRW1,152.5 and Wednesday's close of KRW1,153.  \par \par \uc2 "Everyone's turning frantically dollar-long from both offshore and onshore now that the fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks more like a reality," said a local bank dealer.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar/won, that has kept to a narrow range since late May, slipped briefly below KRW1,150 earlier this week on expectations of an easing in Seoul's efforts to weaken the won to support exports amid growing price pressure from higher energy costs.  \par \par \uc2 -By Hye-Seung Seo, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165; hyeseung.seo@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Shishir Mudgil [ 15-07-04 0223GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040715e07f0001k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
