\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korea poll to cut risks, no big policy shift seen.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoo Choonsik  \par \uc2 621 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2004\par 18:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, April 15 (Reuters) - The expected victory by the pro-government Uri Party in South Korea's parliamentary election on Thursday should cut political risks hindering the economy, but is not seen having a big impact on economic policy or markets.  \par \par \uc2 The political turbulence created by the impeachment of President Roh Moo-hyun last month by the opposition-controlled parliament created a new headache for Asia's fourth-largest economy already struggling to cope with weak local consumption.  \par \par \uc2 "If the political uncertainty eases soon, that would be good for business confidence and for foreign investment," Rob Subbaraman, senior Asia economist at Lehman Brothers in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 But he added: "The Korean economy is run by the ministries, technocrats, and so I do not expect it (the election) to have a major impact on the economy."  \par \par \uc2 Foreign investors and the opposition Grand National Party have said they were concerned the centre-left Uri Party, which expects to have won 151 or 152 seats in the 299-seat National Assembly, could use a majority to indulge militant labour.  \par \par \uc2 But Chung Sye-kyun, Uri's top policymaker, said his party would be asking foreign businesses what they needed to invest more in South Korea.  \par \par \uc2 "We will be showing that we are committed to letting the market work and that there will be continuity in policy without dramatic changes," he told Reuters soon after exit polls.  \par \par \uc2 International ratings agency Fitch Ratings was broadly positive on the election.  \par \par \uc2 "Perhaps, the Uri-dominated parliament will press ahead with corporate governance and reform. We also expect a more effective legislative body than we've had in nine to 12 months," said Brian Coulton, senior director for Asian sovereign ratings.  \par \par \uc2 President Roh came to power in February, 2003, pledging to curb the power of the sprawling family-run conglomerates, known as "chaebol", smooth labour relations and make the country into a regional business hub.  \par \par \uc2 But tangible progress has been limited with Roh facing an opposition-dominated parliament and distractions ranging from a crisis over North Korea's nuclear aims to a bursting local consumer debt bubble and, most recently, the impeachment crisis.  \par \par \uc2 STOCKS SEEN UP ON VOTE  \par \par \uc2 Major South Korean stocks trading overseas were trading mixed after early results of the vote. Market analysts said the local stock market was expected to get an initial boost on Friday on hopes for political stability, but as in the case of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds U.S. trends were likely to set the pace thereafter.  \par \par \uc2 "The Uri Party's win is not likely to influence the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The dollar/yen rate and dollar supply/demand here are more important factors", said a dealer at KorAm bank.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealers say \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may ease heavy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to keep\par the won             from strengthening against the U.S. dollar after\par the election.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But they say a change in a policy, which has been aimed at maintaining the competitiveness of South Korean exports, would be due to worries a weak won could fan mounting inflationary pressure rather than a change in parliamentary leadership.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brisk sales abroad of computer chips, cars and mobile handsets pushed South Korea's exports up 39 percent in the first quarter from a year before, and that prompted the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to upgrade last week its economic growth forecast.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts have called for more foreign direct investment and stronger domestic consumption to provide a buffer for when cyclical export markets falter.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But they concede there do not appear to be many options for the government to give an immediate boost to consumption without creating negative side-effects on inflation and other fronts. (Additional reporting by Jean Yoon).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040415e04f002ek\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
