\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Hurt by weak yen, U.S. rate view.  \b0\par\par\uc2 506 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2004\par 05:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Vidya Ranganathan\par     SINGAPORE, April 15 (Reuters) - Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell\par further on Thursday, hurting from weakness in the Japanese yen\par and from stronger U.S. economic data, although \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par analysts said this bout of weakness in the regionals would soon\par be over.\par     The U.S. dollar got a boost from data showing an\par unexpectedly sharp rise in U.S. consumer prices and a narrowing\par of the huge trade deficit.\par     The Japanese yen        lost two percent on Wednesday and\par fell close to 109 per dollar. Analysts said portfolio outflows\par from Japan, adjustments in the massive long yen positions\par against the euro and dollar and the U.S. data weighed on the\par yen.\par     Its decline was the biggest influence on regionals.\par     The Singapore dollar        was down over two cents or 1.3\par percent from Tuesday's four-year peak. It fell to 1.6830/40 per\par dollar, having pared all the gains it made on the back of a\par surprise monetary tightening by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Monday.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     The Taiwan dollar          weakened to 33.16 per dollar,\par down more than a percent from Tuesday's three-year highs.\par     "This is not a sustained dollar upturn," said Bhanu Baweja,\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist with UBS.\par     "I think the appreciation pressure on the yen will continue\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par because the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been diluted," he said, citing\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's deepening economic recovery as the reason why the\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japanese were comfortable with a stronger yen.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And, the story in the rest of Asia was similar. Economic\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par growth was broadening in most countries, except \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -weakening \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by authorities was\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par moderating, he said.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Philippine peso  was marginally weak and the\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Indonesian rupiah  was down too at 8,630/8,635 per\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par dollar.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Markets in Thailand were closed for the Songkran or new\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par year holiday and Korean markets were shut for parliamentary\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Stock markets in the region too were weak after the fall in\par U.S. shares, adding to the pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Traders said there would be bids for Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  once\par markets sensed the dollar's upward momentum was easing.\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par  Change on the day at   0352 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       108.52         107.17       -1.24\par  Sing dlr        1.6836         1.6733       -0.61\par  Taiwan dlr      33.015         32.920       -0.29\par  Korean won     1157.00        1151.30       -0.49\par  B