\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-S.Korea  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  says no policy change on won.  \b0\par\par\uc2 374 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2004\par 08:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SEOUL, April 8 (Reuters) - South Korea had not changed its current policy on the won despite a growing current account surplus, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said on Thursday, indicating it would continue to favour keeping the won low.   \par \par \uc2  Bank of Korea Assistant Governor Park Jae-hwan told reporters the bank's views on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not changed because of booming exports, which prompted it to upgrade this year's current account surplus to $15 billion from the previous $6 billion.   \par \par \uc2  "Our views are the same because the U.S. economy is growing much faster than had been expected and there are also other accounts to look after other than the current account balance," Park told reporters.   \par \par \uc2  A weaker won helps keep South Korean exports more price competitive in international markets.   \par \par \uc2  But there has been growing speculation that South Korea has been loosening its grip on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  given that exports have been booming and on the view that a firmer won could help contain inflationary pressures coming from higher global raw material prices.   \par \par \uc2  The won has risen 4.6 percent against the dollar so far this year.   \par \par \uc2  The latest comments came hours after the finance ministry announced a plan to offer one trillion won ($877.9 million) in five-year treasuries next week to fund the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  As in the case of most other countries, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  rarely confirms \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but has admitted to conducting what it terms are "smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 " to stabilise the market in the face of speculative trading.   \par \par \uc2  Economists and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers say South Korea, mindful of the still fragile base of its economic recovery, has spent billions of dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market containing the won's rise.   \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Thursday, the Bank of Korea raised its forecast for this year's economic growth by more than a half percentage point to as high as six percent from a previous forecast for about 5.2 percent rise.   \par \par \uc2  But Bank of Korea Governor Park Seung said domestic consumption remained stubbornly weak and the recovery was largely driven by booming exports, supported by strong demand from China.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040408e048000rw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
