\par\par \b \uc2 S Korea Shifting  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Policy To Spur Consumption -StanChart  \b0\par\par\uc2 586 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2004\par 05:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Authorities in \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  are gradually scaling back \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the won, mirroring policy shifts underway in other parts of Asia including Japan and India, said Standard Chartered regional economist Mike Moran.  \par \par \uc2 The won has risen sharply in recent weeks against the dollar, helped by foreign capital flows into Korea and a slightly less \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  approach by the local \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy. Korean markets are shut Monday for a holiday, but the spot dollar ended Friday at KRW1,141.7, near its lowest level since November 2000, and down from around KRW1,200 at the start of the year.  \par \par \uc2 "Official resistance to a stronger won is...gradually receding," Moran said in a research report released Monday. "Rising external balances and higher commodity prices have prompted a reevaluation of recent \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy."  \par \par \uc2 In a research report, Moran said a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will help neutralize import price pressures, allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep interest rates low to support the domestic economy.  \par \par \uc2 But the keyword for policy-makers, he said, remains "gradual," adding that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue if volatility remains high.  \par \par \uc2 A policy of less aggressive yen sales was reflected in data issued Friday by the Bank of Korea. It said South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves reached $163.56 billion at the end of March, up from $163 billion at the end of February. Selling won for dollars builds \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which explains the sharp increase in Korea's reserves since September.  \par \par \uc2 Moran said the gradual shift in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy reflects the two-speed nature of the Korean economy, with exports strong but domestic demand still relatively anemic.  \par \par \uc2 "Diverging trends in external and domestic growth remain well-entrenched," he said. "The strength of Korea's export sectors are having little discernible impact on the domestic economy (due) to the increasingly capital-intensive structure of local industry and smaller proportion of total jobs being accounted for by this sector."  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, while Korea's chaebols, the local name given to conglomerates, continue to report strong earnings, domestic small and medium-sized enterprises are still struggling. "Their greater dependence on the domestic economic cycle is keeping upside risks for this sector limited," Moran said.  \par \par \uc2 One obstacle to a recovery in domestic demand is public discontent about political developments - such as the impeachment proceedings against President Roh Moo-hyun - ahead of parliamentary elections later this months.  \par \par \uc2 "Public discontent could weaken confidence in the short run, delaying our initial timetable for a consumption recovery. Following our recent country visit, the 'feel good factor' is still clearly lacking and a significant rebound in spending may not emerge until year-end," said Moran.  \par \par \uc2 He said the political tug-of-war over impeachment is being won by the pro-government Uri Party. Public support for the impeachment has remained damagingly low for the opposition, said the economist, allowing the Uri Party to boost its support by tapping public discontent.  \par \par \uc2 Roh has vowed to step down if the Uri Party fares poorly in the ballot. Major newspaper polls published earlier this week predicted the party would win a majority in the National Assembly.  \par \par \uc2 "For the opposition parties, the decision to push for Roh's impeachment could turn out to be their biggest political miscalculation, costing them their parliamentary majority come April 15," said Moran.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Benjamin Pedley, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4044; ben.pedley@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Mary de Wet [ 05-04-04 0336GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040405e0450003k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
