\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Won near one-month highs, yen helps.  \b0\par\par\uc2 739 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 04:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Ovais Subhani\par     SINGAPORE, March 17 (Reuters) - The South Korean won\par advanced on Wednesday to peaks not seen in about a month as\par Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were buoyed by the Japanese yen's rise to a\par three-week high against the U.S. dollar.\par     The won            rose to a high of 1,166.3 to the dollar,\par its highest since February 20, defying a warning by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that political uncertainty stemming from the impeachment\par of President Roh Moo-hyun might delay an already fragile\par recovery in Asia's fourth-largest economy.\par     "If uncertainty over the impeachment lingers, weak\par consumption and slow investment could delay economic recovery\par and hurt employment," the Bank of Korea said late on Tuesday.\par     Dealers in Singapore said the market instead focused on\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves data showing a modest fall in\par holdings in the first half of March, providing evidence that\par authorities might be more willing to tolerate a stronger\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , like much of export-driven Asia, has been\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heavily to keep its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from appreciating and\par hurting competitiveness against the backdrop of the U.S.\par dollar's two-year downtrend.\par  \par     But rising global raw material prices in recent months have\par threatened to fuel inflation through costly imports.\par     A hike in interest rates, typically used to fight\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 inflation, is seen as hurting already weak domestic consumption  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 and investment. Therefore the argument that a stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 could help curb imported inflation has surfaced in recent  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 comments by top South Korean finance ministry and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 officials.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But that discussion was sidelined last Friday when Roh was  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 impeached by the opposition-controlled parliament for violating  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 an election law and the won fell to a one-week low of 1,181.5  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 amid fears of political turmoil.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said on Tuesday, South Korea's  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 financial markets have largely absorbed the shock and  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 stabilised for the time-being.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Possibility of a wider fallout appears slim," said James  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malcolm, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase, in his morning  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 notes on Wednesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said while finance ministry officials continue to  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 reiterate their intent to curb \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market volatility, they  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 have also shown a more relaxed stance to recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gains.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 YEN LEADS  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere, the firmer yen played a major role in pushing  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  higher.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Singapore dollar touched 1.6990 a dollar in New  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 York, its strongest since March 2, before fears of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  prompted players to book profits on gains.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Sing dollar rose as high as 1.7010 per dollar in early  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian trade on Wednesday. Non-oil exports, due at 0500 GMT, are  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 forecast to have risen six percent from January and 20.7  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 percent from 12 month earlier.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     The Thai baht        rose to around 39.41 per dollar, from\par Tuesday's one-week low at 39.53.\par     Dealers said the baht's upside was limited by recent stock\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 market losses. Foreign investors were net sellers of Thai  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 stocks on Tuesday for the third straight session, taking total  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 net sales to 6.2 billion baht.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     The Taiwan dollar          rose to its highest in two weeks\par to 33.302 per U.S. dollar, before giving up some of the gains.\par     The Indonesian rupiah        consolidated its position on\par the stronger side of the 8,600 per dollar level.\par     The Philippine peso        edged up to a high of 56.20 per\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 dollar, from Tuesday's low at 56.30, despite news that the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 government's tax agency had missed its revenue collection  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 target in February.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Change on the day 0255 GMT   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\p