\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-S.Korea says sharp  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  volatility should be curbed.   \b0\par\par\uc2 255 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 10:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, March 16 (Reuters) - Excess volatility in South Korea's exchange rate should be curbed, a senior finance ministry official said on Tuesday, suggesting authorities were again focusing their attention on keeping the won competitive. Allowing a market-determined exchange rate was only possible in major markets like the United States, said Choi Joong-kyung, chief of the Finance Ministry's international finance bureau, suggesting the smaller Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was more susceptible to wild speculative swings.   \par \par \uc2 "Sharp volatility (of dollar/won) in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate should be prevented," Choi told a seminar.   \par \par \uc2 He said the authorities would monitor the market for the time being, unless movements in the exchange rate hurt the stability of the economy.   \par \par \uc2 His comments appeared to contrast with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief's statement last week that the market should dictate the exchange rate.   \par \par \uc2 Like other export-dependent Asian countries, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily and bought dollars to stop its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rising for fear of hurting a still fragile economic recovery.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par This policy kept the won  nearly flat against the dollar last year\par while the Japanese yen        gained over 10 percent versus the U.S.\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . South Korea and Japan compete in major export markets.\par The won             rose for a second session on Tuesday against the\par dollar, ending local trade at 1,170.7, compared with Monday's close of\par 1,175.3. It has gained 1.8 percent against the dollar so far this\par year.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040316e03g0019p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
