\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-S.Korea signals softer  \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange \highlight\uc2  line.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoo Choonsik  \par \uc2 589 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 12:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, March 3 (Reuters) - Remarks by South Korea's new finance minister appeared on Wednesday to provide the latest sign authorities will allow a gradual appreciation of the won.  \par \par \uc2 Lee Hun-jai seemed to diverge subtly from his predecessor when answering a question on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy put to him at a lunch meeting of foreign reporters.  \par \par \uc2 "The exchange rate will be decided by supply and demand and economic fundamentals", said Lee, who was appointed in early February.  \par \par \uc2 Former finance minister Kim Jin-pyo had said the won's rate "should" be decided by the market and economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 Lee's remark indicated market forces would be given more emphasis in determining won levels against the U.S. dollar, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Like other Asian countries, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent billions of dollars \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in an effort to stop its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rising. A rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes exports less competitive on world markets.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the fourth-largest economy in Asia, has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  as foreign investment in local stocks and mounting current account surpluses sucked in dollars.  \par \par \uc2 Thanks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the won remained almost flat against the dollar between the start and end of 2003. The yen and the euro gained more than 10 percent and nearly 20 percent, respectively.  \par \par \uc2 The won ended at 1,177 per dollar on Wednesday, up 1.26 percent from the end of 2003.  \par \par \uc2 Lee's remark came shortly after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data suggested government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the won had weakened.  \par \par \uc2 "The comment was made as dealers are already sensing some changes in the government's attitude," said a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a local bank. "Dealers say the government has become more flexible even when \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 Market speculation over a shift in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy began to grow when the finance ministry abruptly rescinded some restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward trading by local financial institutions. The ministry announced the move a week after Lee took office and a month after the controls had been imposed.  \par \par \uc2 In a further sign the government's attitude could be changing, data on Wednesday showed a marked slowdown in South Korea's foreign reserves growth.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's figures showed reserves rose only $420 million in the second half of February, after rising $5.14 billion in the first 15 days.  \par \par \uc2 MISLEADING?  \par \par \uc2 Some dealers said, however, it was too soon to say South Korea was ditching a policy of keeping the won cheap against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , noting authorities may have felt they could not fight the tide of a strengthening dollar in the past month.  \par \par \uc2 Rising international prices for oil and other key raw materials have also forced South Korea to adopt a lower profile in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as a further weakening in the won would inflate import bills, they said.  \par \par \uc2 "The government is in a situation where it can not actively push the dollar higher against the won mainly because of the heavy pressure on prices from strong raw materials prices," a dealer at a foreign bank said.  \par \par \uc2 Lee said he would stick to policies promoting economic growth because the recovery was still fragile. The country has kept interest rates at record lows since July to try to boost the economy, which fell briefly into recession in mid-2003.  \par \par \uc2 He predicted 2004 economic growth of five to six percent. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has forecast 2004 gross domestic product of 5.2 percent after an estimated 2.9 percent in 2003.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040303e033001s8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
