\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-S.Korea reserves surge, heavy  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  cited.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoo Choonsik  \par \uc2 478 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2004\par 10:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Feb 17 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  released data on Tuesday showing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves surged by more than $5 billion in just two weeks, providing what dealers said was clear evidence of the extent of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.   \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Korea said on Tuesday foreign reserves, valued in U.S. dollars, rose to a record $162.6 billion by mid-February from $157.5 billion at the end of January. It listed a stronger euro against the U.S. dollar as the key factor.   \par \par \uc2  But local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said the increase reflected \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep the won from strengthening too much versus the dollar at a time when exports are the driving force for economic recovery.   \par \par \uc2  "We believe some 70 to 80 percent of the increase came from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar buying in the market," said a dealer at a local bank. "We even think authorities had bought close to $1 billion every day at least during the first week."   \par \par \uc2  Once faced with a sovereign debt default in late 1997 after massive outflows of foreign capitals had left it with less than $4 billion in reserves, South Korea now boasts the world's fourth-largest reserves behind Japan, China and Taiwan.   \par \par \uc2  Senior officials at various government authorities would not comment on the market talk, maintaining that a stronger euro was the main factor. The euro gained two percent against the U.S. dollar in the first weeks of February.   \par \par \uc2  But even if South Korea held half of its reserves in euros, the rise in the euro would have pushed up the dollar value of the country's reserves by about $1.6 billion. South Korea has never said how much of its reserves are held in euro assets.   \par \par \uc2  Assuming South Korea holds 20 percent of its reserves in euros - the IMF said euros made up 14.6 percent of global reserve holdings at the end of 2002 - the stronger euro would have seen the value of euro holdings rise by about $600 million.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials said gains on its investment in unspecified assets had also pushed up the reserves. But even a six percent gain per annum - which is a tough task - would have generated only about $390 million at the most.   \par \par \uc2  Some dealers believe virtually all of the increase in reserves came from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the market, effectively keeping the won flat against the dollar between the end of 2002 and the end of 2003, while the yen gained about 11 percent and the euro about 20 percent against the dollar, respectively.   \par \par \uc2  During the same 12-month period, the country's foreign reserves rose $34 billion. (Additional reporting by Jeon Chong-woo and Kim Yeon-hee).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040217e02h001mu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
