\par\par \b \uc2 Most Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Up Late; Central Banks Battle G7  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Ron Harui  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 762 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2004\par 09:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose against the dollar Wednesday as investors tested whether the region's central banks could withstand a push from the Group of Seven powers for them to stop restraining their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Several Asian central banks bought dollars to fight the rising tide of their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but they couldn't overcome waves of selling as the G7's weekend call for more Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "flexibility" sank in, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 For a second day, the Singapore dollar notched a four-year high and the Korean won also gained, leading the region's advance on a wave of what traders called speculative dollar-selling. Analysts said Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely keep grinding higher in coming days.  \par \par \uc2 The G7's message "is clearly supportive of Asian" \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Patrick Bennett, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank. But the region's monetary authorities "will continue their own policies to pursue competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates to ensure external demand."  \par \par \uc2 Finance Ministers and central bankers from the G7 - the U.S., Japan, Germany, France, the U.K., Italy and Canada - reiterated Saturday that they want freer exchange rates, specifying they want more flexibility where it is currently lacking. They also said that "excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth." Traders take the statement to mean the G7 wants Asian central banks to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise to take some of the pressure of the dollar's global fall off the soaring euro.  \par \par \uc2 The central banks of Singapore, Taiwan and South Korea were apparently buying dollars in the market Wednesday, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Sameer Goel, regional strategist at Bank of America, said Asian central banks are unlikely to concede to strengthening in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the short term, though the central banks may allow gradual and grudging appreciation this year.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Trade Representative Robert Zoellick Wednesday renewed Washington's call for China - which the U.S. views as the chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator - to loosen the yuan's peg to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "One of the issues we're going to have to deal with over time is the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Zoellick told reporters during a Tokyo visit. China "must give a fair shake to others including U.S. producers."  \par \par \uc2 On Monday Beijing strongly denied a weekend report that it would widen the yuan's trading band, allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise within the next couple of months.  \par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 , another persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervener\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official defended Seoul's aggressive attempts to support the dollar to prevent the won - and thus Korean exports - from becoming too expensive.  \par \par \uc2 Seoul prefers a weak won to help support exports because domestic consumption isn't picking up quickly enough despite a recovery in the global economy, said Yeung-Kyun Rhee, director general of the Bank of Korea's international department.  \par \par \uc2 Rhee, in a recent interview with Dow Jones Newswires, sought to put the ball in Tokyo's court, saying G7 member Japan's own yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are helping Seoul subdue the won. "If the Japanese government continues to block the yen's rise, then there is a limit to how much the won can rise," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials have stressed since the weekend G7 meeting in Boca Raton, Fla., that the call for more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility doesn't apply to them and that they, too, will keep propping up the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 While Asian central banks are likely to continue their heavy dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they could face difficulties in keeping their exchange rates competitive in the overseas markets down the road, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of America expects faster economic growth in Asia, a change in the yuan peg and other factors to increase the impetus for appreciation in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year.  \par \par \uc2 Around 0808 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted at KRW1,160.6, down from Tuesday's KRW1,162.1 close. Against the Singapore unit, it was at S$1.6747, down from S$1.6758 late in Asia Tuesday, and versus the rupiah it was at 8,399 rupiah, lower from its close Tuesday at IDR8,400.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Thai baht, the dollar was quoted at THB38.97, up from THB38.94 Tuesday. It fell against the New Taiwan dollar, to NT$33.147 from NT$33.195, but was marginally higher against the Philippine peso, at PHP55.905, up from PHP55.845.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 6415 4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com \par    -Edited by William Mallard \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 1136 4250  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040211e02b000ah\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\p