\par\par \b \uc2 Bk Of Korea Aide:Japan  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Policy Affects Won's Direction  \b0\par\par\uc2 By In Kyung Kim  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 879 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2004\par 07:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--Japan's aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sustain its economic recovery may have the knock-on effect of helping South Korea in its efforts to hold down the value of the won and support its own export-led recovery, a Bank of Korea official said.  \par \par \uc2 "If the Japanese government continues to block the yen's rise, then there is a limit to how much the won can rise," Yeung-Kyun Rhee, director general of the Bank of Korea's international department, told Dow Jones Newswires in a recent interview.  \par \par \uc2 He also predicted that the dollar's weakness against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the won, won't continue for long given the aggressiveness of Japanese efforts to defend the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Rhee said Seoul prefers a weak won to help support exports because domestic consumption isn't picking up quickly enough despite a recovery in the global economy.  \par \par \uc2 "The Korean economy isn't recovering as fast as other economies. In that aspect, the local won cannot strengthen at the same pace as other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Rhee said South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy remains unchanged despite a call by the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations for Asian countries to allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen. G7 finance ministers met in Boca Raton, Florida over the weekend.  \par \par \uc2 "We are relieved by the G7 statement. It remains surprisingly unchanged from September. Japan, the euro zone and the U.S. have all incorporated their views in the statement," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Rhee indicated that South Korean authorities weren't greatly worried about the won's gains following the G7 meeting, saying they weren't as significant as those seen after the previous G7 meeting that initially called for more flexibility in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "We have kept an eye on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations and there haven't been any rapid rises or falls since the G7 meeting," he said. "Sure, the dollar fell but it's not like plunges of 10-plus won like after the previous G7."  \par \par \uc2 At 0612 GMT, the dollar was trading at 1,160.6 won, down 0.6% from a close of KRW1,168.1 Friday before the G7 released its communique over the weekend.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. unit had lost 1.5% in the three trading days following the last G7 meeting Sept. 22.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par       Tighter NDF Rules Aimed At Speculation, Volatility \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On new restrictions recently imposed in the non-deliverable forward market, Rhee said the regulations were aimed at protecting the market from speculative forces and excessive volatility, but that the authorities need more time to determine whether the move will prove effective.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Last month, the South Korean government tightened regulations on trading in the NDF market. The multi-pronged move was said by many \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders to be an attempt by the government to slow the won's rise against the dollar in order to keep exports competitive.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rhee said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will continue to focus on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate rather than inflation for the time being.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Aside from the forthcoming elections, there are concerns about inflation due to rises in crude oil and global raw material prices, but it's premature to be worried about inflation," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On whether \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be more open like Japan about its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, Rhee said there is no rule that prevents South Korea from doing so but that it is unlikely to happen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Japan publicly announces the size of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. There's no rule that says Korea can't do that, but the dollar-won market is very small and we can't behave like developed countries," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rhee declined to comment on what defines excessive volatility for the South Korean government.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's no fixed criterion for determining what a rapid rise or fall in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate is. It depends on the situation. For example, the dollar fell to Y112 from Y115 after the previous G7 meeting. In such a situation, there's no way that we can prevent the dollar from falling against the won," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There would have been no way to reverse the trend even if we had poured in all the money available," he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rhee also rejected criticism of the government's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Smoothing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is different from controlling the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate. Japan poured in $70 billion so far this year, but no one says Japan manipulates the market."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He stressed that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has sufficient resources to step into the market if necessary and downplayed speculation among \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealers that South Korean authorities have started to run out of funds to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's an unlimited amount of money piled up in the vault," Rhee said. "Unlike the government which has to issue bonds to raise funds to stabilize the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can do it unnoticed because it can desterilize money supply through issuance of monetary stabilization bonds."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -In Kyung Kim, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165; in-kyung.kim@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by George B. Thomas  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040211e02b0006c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
