\par\par \b \uc2 S Korea Govt Official: Expect Stronger Dlr On G7 Mtg  \b0\par\par\uc2 418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2004\par 01:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The South Korean government expects the pressure on the won to strengthen against the U.S. dollar to ease due to the statement by the Group of Seven industrial powers over the weekend, an official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy said Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "We think the meeting results could reverse the dollar's weakening trend," Yoon Yeo-Kwon, director of the ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market division, told Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 Yoon interpreted the new language on exchange rates in the statement following the G7 meeting in Florida as specifically referring to the U.S dollar.  \par \par \uc2 After the two-day meeting, G7 finance ministers and central bankers warned in a statement that "excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth."  \par \par \uc2 In a separate phone interview, Lee Jae-Ouk, assistant governor at the Bank of Korea, said post-G7 comment by Japan's finance minister would likely affect \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies of other Asian countries.  \par \par \uc2 "We don't expect significant changes in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market due to the G7...but Japan's (post-G7) moves will likely influence other Asian countries, including us," said Lee.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese finance minister Sadakuza Tanigaki said after the G7 meeting, "The newly inserted words in the statement, such as 'excessive volatility' and 'disorderly moves,' are what I myself and Europe have been suggesting to be included."  \par \par \uc2 Monday morning, before the official market opening, the Bank of Japan is suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market by selling yen.  \par \par \uc2 Like Japan, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  has justified dollar-buying market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as "smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 " to ease the volatility of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Yoon of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy predicted the G7's renewed call for "more flexibility in exchange rates" won't likely disrupt the won as it did after the meeting in Dubai in September.  \par \par \uc2 From the last G7 meeting to last Friday, the won has gained 2.9% versus the dollar to KRW1,168.1. At 0130 GMT, the dollar had fallen further to KRW1,167.5, reflecting the belief of many players that the G7 statement won't change the dollar's direction in the long term.  \par \par \uc2 Yoon maintained the Seoul government will continue with its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy to help sustain strong exports until there is a change in "economic fundamentals."  \par \par \uc2 -By Hye-Seung Seo, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165; hyeseung.seo@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Mary de Wet  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040209e0290000r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
