\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Peso wins reprieve, rest cautious.  \b0\par\par\uc2 762 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2004\par 10:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Ovais Subhani\par     SINGAPORE, Feb 5 (Reuters) - The Philippine peso rose more\par than half a percent on Thursday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  raised\par the amount of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves banks are required to hold with\par it.\par     The peso        jumped to 55.85 per dollar, from near\par record lows of 56.22 in early trade, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  raised\par the liquidity reserve requirement to 10 percent from eight\par percent.\par     It was the bank's first policy change since last August and\par is expected to drain 30 billion pesos from the financial system\par -- effectively a monetary tightening.\par     Dealers in Manila said there had been some rumours about\par the move early in the day, but most players were caught off\par guard.\par     Analysts said given the bleak outlook for the peso amid\par worries over presidential elections in May, a mix of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and policy measures by authorities to support the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should be expected.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "As we approach the elections and the market begins to\par price in the inherent risks, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will come in to\par implement some sort of change in the trading regime in order to\par stem the tide," said Simon Flint, strategist at Bank of America\par in Singapore.\par     Dealers in Manila said the move would provide temporary\par support to the peso as banks cut back on dollar purchases and\par square long dollar positions to meet the new reserve\par requirement.\par  \par     Analysts fear the upcoming elections could compound the\par country's fiscal and balance of payments problems.\par     Moody's Investors Service last week lowered its long-term\par ratings for the Philippines, saying unstable election year\par politics threatened to worsen the country's budget woes.\par     G7 FOCUS\par     Elsewhere, most Asian regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  posted cautious\par gains ahead of a Group of Seven industrial nations' two-day\par meeting starting on Friday.\par     Dealers said bullish sentiment on regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would\par remain intact for the medium term given the improved economic\par outlook for the year.\par     But few were now looking for strong near-term gains as the\par market has scaled back hopes of a tough stand by the G7 against\par Asia's policy of resisting appreciation versus the U.S. dollar.\par     Analysts saw the meeting of G7 finance ministers and\par central bankers in Florida as still fraught with risks of a\par surprise and advised caution on taking fresh positions.\par     "The medium-term outlook remains unchanged. Strategy-wise\par you would want to maintain short dollar/Asia," said James\par Malcolm, strategist at JP Morgan Chase in Singapore.\par     "Perception around the G7 has been scaled back but nobody\par is sure. There could be an upset on comments on the sidelines."\par     Taiwan and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wednesday to curb gains in their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  underlined market  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 fears that Asian central banks would stick to their policy  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 stance.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Taiwan dollar was steady but stayed off  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wednesday's 18-month high of 33.23 per U.S. dollar. The South   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Korean won             ended local trade at levels just\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 shy of a four-month high of 1,164.8 per dollar hit on Tuesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Singapore dollar rose to 1.6920 per U.S. dollar  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 but dealers said players were in no mood to test recent highs  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 on the stronger side of the 1.69 per dollar level after some  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 local banks were spotted buying U.S. dollars.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Thai baht edged close to the 39 per dollar  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 level, recovering from the hit it took last week from worries  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 about the impact of bird flu on the world's fourth-biggest  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 chicken exporter.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The flu, which has spurred the culling of millions of  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 chickens, claimed its fifth human victim in Thailand on  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wednesday and its 11th in Vietnam on Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Change on the day 0850 GMT   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       105.42         105.40       -0.02\par  Sing dlr        1.6921         1.6943       +0.13\par  Taiwan dlr      33.285         33.288       +0.01\par  Korean won     1166.00        1168.20       +0.19\par  Baht             39.03          39.08       +0.13\par  Peso             55.90          56.16       +0.47\par  Rupiah         8445.00        8445.00       +0.00\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\