\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Steady on uncertainty, fear ahead of G7.   \b0\par\par\uc2 579 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2004\par 04:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Ovais Subhani\par     SINGAPORE, Feb 5 (Reuters) - Most Asian regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par were steady on Thursday as markets geared up for a Group of\par Seven industrial nations' meeting.\par     Dealers said bullish sentiment on regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par remained intact for the medium term given the improved economic\par outlook for the year.\par     But there was little expectation of strong near-term gains\par and major players were looking to trim short dollar positions\par as they scaled back hopes of a tough stand by the G7 against\par Asia's policy of resisting appreciation versus the U.S. dollar.\par     Analysts saw the two-day meeting of G7 finance ministers\par and central bankers in Florida, ending on Saturday, as still\par fraught with risks of a surprise and advised caution on taking\par fresh positions.\par     "The medium-term outlook remains unchanged. Strategy-wise\par you would want to maintain short dollar/Asia," said James\par Malcolm, strategist at JP Morgan Chase in Singapore.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     "Perception around the G7 has been scaled back but nobody\par is sure. There could be an upset on comments on the sidelines."\par     ASIA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  POLICY\par     Taiwan and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wednesday to curb gains in their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  underlined market   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 fears that Asian central banks would stick to their policy   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 stance.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Taiwan dollar was steady but stayed off   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wednesday's 18-month high of 33.23 per U.S. dollar. The South    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Korean won             rose but was still some distance\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 from a four-month high of 1,164.8 per dollar hit on Tuesday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Singapore dollar held around 1.6940 per U.S.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 dollar but dealers said players were in no mood to test recent   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 highs on the stronger side of the 1.69 per dollar level after   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 some local banks were spotted buying U.S. dollars on Wednesday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Thai baht edged close to the 39 per dollar   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 level, recovering from the hit it took last week from worries   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 about the impact of bird flu on the world's fourth-biggest   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 chicken exporter.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The flu, which has spurred the culling of millions of   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 chickens, claimed its fifth human victim in Thailand on   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wednesday and its 11th in Vietnam on Thursday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bewildered Philippine peso took a breather as it   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 continued to recover from last week's all-time low of 56.22 hit   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 in the wake of a credit ratings downgrade.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dealers in Manila said worries over presidential elections   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 in May would keep the peso under pressure, but talk of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central   \f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  support at around 56.20 and reduced demand for dollars   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 from companies could help in the short term.   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Change on the day 0255 GMT    \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       105.49         105.40       -0.09\par  Sing dlr        1.6938         1.6943       +0.03\par  Taiwan dlr      33.291         33.288       -0.01\par  Korean won     1167.30        1168.20       +0.08\par  Baht             39.05          39.08       +0.08\par  Peso             56.18          56.16       -0.04\par  Rupiah         8455.00        8445.00       -0.12\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       105.49         107.31       +1.73\par  Sing dlr        1.6938         1.6978       +0.24\par  Taiwan dlr      33.291         33.978       +2.06\par  Korean won     1167.30        1192.00       +2.12\par  Baht             39.05          39.59       +1.37\par  Peso             56.18          55.49       -1.23\par  Rupiah         8455.00        8410.00       -0.53.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040205e025000kh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
