\par\par \b \uc2 S Korea Govt: Concerned About Recent  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Volatility  \b0\par\par\uc2 320 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2004\par 00:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--In its second verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy warned Wednesday that it will take "necessary" action to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 "The government is concerned about recent excessive volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. It will closely watch the market situation to take appropriate action," said the ministry in a statement issued minutes before the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market opened for trading.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar slipped to 1,176.2 won Tuesday from its previous close of KRW1,180.5, and well off a Dec. 26 close of KRW1,200 despite measures by the authorities to slow the won's rise.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    The last time the dollar traded at such levels was two weeks ago when the government then issued a verbal warning, quickly followed by a new regulation to cap offshore investors' dollar sales via the nondeliverable forward market.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the curbs on the NDF market had only a short-term impact and the won marched up, pressured by a surge in foreign fund inflows to the local stock market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wednesday's verbal warning didn't stop the dollar falling further at the open. At 0055 GMT, the dollar was down at KRW1,174.3, a level last seen in mid-November, as exporters offloaded the U.S. unit for month-end purposes.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market just doesn't think the government has much more to do to change the big picture - the won's eventual appreciation," said one dealer.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So far this year, the government has issued KRW2 trillion in treasury bonds to raise funds to slow the won's rise.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government has parliament approval to issue up to KRW7.8 trillion in treasurys in 2004 to stabilize the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Hye-Seung Seo, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165; hyeseung.seo@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Simon Harvey  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040127e01r00112\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
