\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Economic recovery to pinch S.Korean trade surplus.  \b0\par\par\uc2 397 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Januar 2004\par 04:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, Jan 1 (Reuters) - South Korea's trade surplus, which expanded 50 percent in 2003 to more than $15 billion, is set to shrink in 2004 as an accelerating economic recovery sucks in more imports, the commerce ministry said on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "Along with a recovery in the global economy, a revival in domestic consumption will keep imports growth at the level of 2003, whereas exports growth will slow to around 10 percent," the ministry said in a statement.  \par \par \uc2 Exports on a customs-clearance basis jumped 19.6 percent in 2003 from a year earlier, powered by chips, automobiles and wireless telecommunications equipment.  \par \par \uc2 Imports increased 17.5 percent, led by crude oil and electronic parts, allowing the country's trade surplus to expand by 50 percent over 2002 to a provisional $15.54 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier in December, Deputy Trade Minister Kim Chil-doo said the trade surplus would narrow 42 percent in 2004 to around $9 billion compared with 2003.  \par \par \uc2 But the continued surplus would put upward pressure on the won, making South Korean exports more expensive.  \par \par \uc2 "A possible appreciation of the Chinese yuan and any move by other countries to step up import restrictions will have a negative impact on our exports," the commerce ministry statement said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in 2003 to keep the won virtually unchanged against the dollar but sending it plunging against the Japanese yen and the euro, which gained 11 percent and 20 percent respectively over the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 In December alone, South Korea posted a trade surplus of $2.3 billion, with exports up 32.5 percent over a year earlier and imports up 22.1 percent. It marked the ninth straight monthly trade surplus.  \par \par \uc2 Soaring exports of cars, chips and mobile phones, especially in late 2003, pulled South Korea's economy out of recession in the third quarter but imports have grown much more slowly because domestic consumption remained in the doldrums.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has forecast South Korea's gross domestic product in 2003 would be 2.9 percent greater than in 2002, when the economy grew 6.3 percent.  \par \par \uc2 An expected 1.1 percent drop in private consumption was attributed for the forecast 2003 economic slowdown, it said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecast the South Korean economy would grow more than five percent in 2004.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040101e011002be\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
