\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korea won up by noon, led by exporters' dlr sales.  \b0\par\par\uc2 402 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2003\par 04:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0300 GMT - South Korea's won             turns firmer by midday, as\par dollar sales by exporters and profit-taking reverses an earlier loss.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Won quoted at 1,184.2 against the dollar after opening at 1,185.0 and compared with Friday's close of 1,185.0, the strongest finish since November 19, when it ended at 1,176.8.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 - Trading thin, but the overall mood positive in the local market, even though the arrest of fugitive Iraqi dictator Saddam Hussein inspires the dollar to rally versus major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 - Against the Japanese yen, the dollar trading at 108.02/08 yen , up from 107.80/90 yen in late New York on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 - Korean government official also says the capture of the fugitive Iraq dictator is likely to lead to a short-term fall in the won against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 - "The won is likely to remain soft in the near term because Saddam Hussein's arrest will help stabilise oil prices and boost the dollar," Kwon Tae-shin, deputy finance minister, says. "But it's hard to predict its long-term effect on the won/dollar."  \par \par \uc2 - However, an increase in dollar supply from exporters and the news that foreign equity investors turn net buyers of local shares by midday help boost the won, dealers say.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par - Seoul shares  up 1.11 percent at 815.02 points, led by Samsung\par Electronics Co Ltd             and Woori Financial Group            .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Dealers say trading will be in a choppy range because the market is also wary of government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is targeting a softer won to maintain the competitiveness of exporters, while sluggish domestic demand puts a brake on economic expansion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - A Bank of Korea official says the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will maintain smoothing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market next year, according to a report on an online news service.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - the Won is expected to move between 1,178-1,190 this week, dealers say.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - The yen/won cross rate , a calculated rate based on each \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's value against the dollar, edges down to 10.9577/9617 from 10.9713/9803 late on Monday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - On the non-deliverable forwards market, one-month won stands at 1,187.7/8.7 versus 1,188.4/9.4 late on Monday and three-month won at 1,1193.0/4.0 versus 1,193.9/4.9.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031215dzcf000mo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
