\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-South Korea looks west to win its won battles.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoo Choonsik   \par \uc2 839 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2003\par 08:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, Nov 28 (Reuters) - For years South Korea has looked east, across the waves to Japan, to help it set \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.   \par \par \uc2 Now the country is looking west, across the Yellow Sea, with an anxious eye on maintaining competitiveness in fast-growing China - its biggest export market and a key competitor.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par To keep the South Korean export machine running, Seoul has spent up to\par $26 billion, by market estimates, buying foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to keep the\par won's value             closely tied to the Chinese yuan, which in turn\par is pegged to the retreating U.S. dollar.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China is the key driver of export growth throughout Asia and by anchoring its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the yuan, South Korea has an advantage over rivals such as Japan whose \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are rising.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But while the won policy may help reflate the South Korean economy, analysts say it could cause fresh problems - including upward pressure on the won when Seoul comes to sell its dollar hoard.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think that the current heavy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would end up with an abrupt fall in the dollar/won rates," said Oh Suk-tae, an economist at Citibank.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the Bank of Korea (BOK) has dumped won on the market, it has been issuing bonds to soak up the money. It's a process known as sterilisation, and it prevents money supply from ballooning and ultimately leading to inflation.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But it also means there's an awful lot of local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt hitting the market through Monetary Stabilisation Bonds (MSBs) and other bonds, which puts upward pressure on interest rates.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BUNDLES OF BONDS   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "As international reserves increase, the BOK sterilises that increase by issuing MSBs. However, there are limits to how much the BOK can issue in MSBs without crowding out other borrowers," said John Chambers, the deputy head of sovereign ratings group at Standard & Poor's.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Unsterilised increases in international reserves provoke an increase in monetary aggregates, which in turn can lead financial agents to anticipate higher prices for assets and eventually for consumer goods," he said.   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  does not admit to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  but analysts say most of a $26 billion rise in government foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves is due to won sales.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sent the won sliding last week when it said it would manage its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy to support exports.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EXPORT ENGINE   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As South Korean mobile phones, computer chips and a host of other goods have been snapped up by Chinese customers, Seoul's trade surplus has surged to its highest in five years.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China this year became South Korea's number one export market. It took 17.9 percent of exports in the 10 months through October, versus 17.6 percent for previous number one, the United States.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There has been nothing short of an explosion in Korea's exports to China, especially since mid-2003," Goldman Sachs analyst Sun-Bae Kim said in a client note issued on November 17.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investment bank Morgan Stanley has estimated that China alone is responsible for 40 percent of South Korean export growth.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite rising demand from South Korean exporters, the heavily managed won has risen less than five percent against the dollar in the past eight months. During the same period, the yen's rise against the dollar was twice that.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The won selling not only protects Seoul's competitiveness in China and the United States. Many other customers also pay in U.S. dollars.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WON STALLED   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Previously the yen and the won had moved in lockstep. Last year dollar/yen fell 11 percent. Dollar/won fell 10 percent.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This year the yen has found favour due to a recovery in Japan. Were the won to move in line with the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , South Korea could risk choking off exports just as its economy climbs out of recession.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Korean government wants to keep the won moving in a very narrow range when the yuan is pegged to the dollar and when the yen is appreciating on the back of the Japanese economy's recovery," Oh said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Both Japan and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  massively but Seoul - operating in a smaller market with daily inter-bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading of around $3 billion - has been more effective.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Policymakers in Seoul, who say they only conduct smoothing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aimed at protecting the market from speculation, argue it's only normal that the won fails to keep pace with the yen.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It is a natural phenomenon that the won has depreciated against the yen recently, reflecting different economic fundamentals in South Korea and Japan," said Deputy Finance Minister Kwon Tae-shin, South Korea's top \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy maker.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists say there's more to it than that.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Export-led growth requires keeping the exchange rate undervalued to repress consumption and promote saving and a high investment rate, which is the source of rapid GDP growth," said Tim Condon, chief regional economist at ING Financial Markets in Hong Kong. ($1=1,202.8 Won).    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031128dzbs001rx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
