\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-S.Korea must prepare for sudden North fail-S&P.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoo Choonsik  \par \uc2 631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 2003\par 10:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Nov 3 (Reuters) - The collapse of North Korea's economy is just a matter of time and South Korea should prepare for an emergency that may cost three times its annual economic output, ratings agency Standard & Poor's said on Monday.   \par \par \uc2  John Chambers, the managing director for sovereign ratings at S&P, said South Korea's sovereign rating was unlikely to change even if North Korea pulled out of talks on Pyongyang's nuclear weapons ambitions.   \par \par \uc2  "In our view, it's only a question of time when North Korea collapses because its current economic model is not sustainable," Chambers told reporters.   \par \par \uc2  He said estimates of the cost of North Korea's economic collapse - and the resultant collapse of the country itself - to the capitalist South ranged from 40 percent to 300 percent of South Korea's annual GDP, a range of $190 billion to $1.4 trillion based on current GDP of about $480 billion.   \par \par \uc2  Some have estimated the cost of South Korea digesting the collapse of the North would exceed an estimated $750 billion bill from the reunification of Germany.   \par \par \uc2  The credit rating agency, like its rivals, sees the North Korean nuclear crisis as a key issue constraining South Korea's credit ratings. S&P has an A-minus long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  credit rating on South Korea.   \par \par \uc2  But Chambers said South Korea's domestic politics could also affect credit ratings because they can influence the government's commitment to pushing ahead with further reforms.   \par \par \uc2  President Roh Moo-hyun shocked the country when he proposed an unprecedented referendum next month on his rule, after a close aide was arrested for taking bribes. The date for the referendum, and even whether it will take place, remain uncertain.   \par \par \uc2  The rating by S&P, whose views are important as they influence the interest rate the country pays on its loans, puts South Korea in the same group as Malaysia, Hungary and Israel.   \par \par \uc2  Although unification has long been a stated goal of South Korea, Seoul has been taken aback by the damage far wealthier West Germany sustained in absorbing East Germany, which was more prosperous than North Korea.   \par \par \uc2  South Korean economists have estimated unification will cost Seoul anything from $200 billion over ten years to six times that sum in a shorter period, depending on the pace of events.   \par \par \uc2  The immediate diplomatic focus is on whether North Korea would join another round of six-country nuclear talks with China, the United States, South Korea, Japan and Russia. Pyongyang agreed in principle last week to resume negotiations.   \par \par \uc2  But Chambers said the matter of greatest importance for South Korea's credit ratings was not the talks themselves. He said the "red line" in the year-old saga was now the North proliferating its nuclear weapons.   \par \par \uc2  On economic reforms, S&P referred to the need for South Korean conglomerates to concentrate on key competencies, for improved corporate governance, lowering the state's participation in banking and making the government's budget more transparent.   \par \par \uc2  It also said building large \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves - South Korea has $141.5 billion at the end of September - had a downside because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had to issue securities to mop up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be better served in improving efficiencies in its own economy to obtain competitiveness with foreign trading partners than in building a high level of international reserves to counter won appreciation," S&P said in a statement.   \par \par \uc2  South Korea's reserves are enough to cover nearly nine months of the country's imports, although \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers in Seoul have said South Korea often bought dollars when the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell against the won.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031103dzb3000e1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
