\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-S.Korea puts brave face on rapid rise in won.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Lee Bong-hyun and Kim Kyoung-wha   \par \uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2003\par 09:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Oct 10 (Reuters) - South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  put on a brave face on Friday over a sustained rise in the won, but analysts cast doubt on whether authorities could control a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation that may threaten an export-led recovery.    \par \par \uc2  In the latest comments aimed at trying to sap the strength of the won, Lee Sung-tae, deputy governor of the Bank of Korea, told Reuters by telephone that a flagging economy should knock some of the wind out of the Korean won .    \par \par \uc2  The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market - faced with mounting dollar supplies from offshore selling, exporter deals and persistent inflows of foreign capital - has seen the won climb more than nine percent against the dollar since a March low.    \par \par \uc2  "It's true that the won is coming under pressure from a current account surplus and foreign buying of domestic stocks," Lee said.    \par \par \uc2  "But there won't be any rapid appreciation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the short run given that the economy is not reviving yet," he added.    \par \par \uc2  The won stood at 1,148.0 at 0700 GMT after opening at 1,149.0. It closed on Thursday at 1,148.6, the strongest finish since November 20, 2000 when it ended at 1,143.7.    \par \par \uc2  Authorities have kept up their rhetoric against the won's advance as it erodes the competitiveness of South Korean products in overseas markets. Exports are a rare bright spot for South Korea's recession-hit economy.    \par \par \uc2  Dealers estimated the government has soaked up more than $3 billion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets this week alone through quasi-government agencies such as state-run banks.    \par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON'S HIT LIST    \par \par \uc2  But it will not be an easy battle for a government facing growing doubts over the effectiveness of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and outside pressure to let market forces dictate movements.    \par \par \uc2  "We'll see how long the government can hold out," said Oh Suk-tae, an economist and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank. "If \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to be justified as a support for future growth, it is worth asking how much incremental growth can Korea expect."    \par \par \uc2  Oh, who expects the won to hit 1,100 per dollar by year-end based on his forecast of 110 yen per dollar, said that the costly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had led to an accumulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which now stand at an all-time high of $141.53 billion.    \par \par \uc2  Compounding the government's worries, a growing number of domestic exporters are opting to settle their export deals at an earlier date on a belief that the won has further to rise.    \par \par \uc2  LG Electronics Inc , South Korea's top home-appliance maker, has converted sizable dollar deposits into the won as part of its hedging strategy.    \par \par \uc2  "We are trying to minimize our dollar exposure," said an official at LG's financing division. "We don't rely on the government's pledge or warnings any longer and have an internal strategy to reshuffle our \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  structure."    \par \par \uc2  Fuelling more concerns over the sustainability of government policy, there has been growing pressure from the United States on Asian countries to allow greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility.    \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  put \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a danger of being tagged as part of a U.S. black list.    \par \par \uc2  "With the Federal Reserve watching more closely and trying to build its case, Seoul may now have to act more cautiously," said Strategic Forecasting Inc, a private Texas-based intelligence firm.    \par \par \uc2  "This could prompt the won to appreciate more quickly, especially if South Korea joins China and Japan atop Washington's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit list," it said in a research note.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031010dzaa000gq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
