\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korea won ends down, losses pared by export deals.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Lee Soo-jung  \par \uc2 559 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2003\par 12:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SEOUL, Sept 8 (Reuters) - The South Korean won         closed lower on\par Monday, tracking the yen's early drop and snapping a three-day winning\par streak, but losses were pared by exporter dollar sales ahead of a local\par holiday, dealers said.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The turn of foreign stock investors to a net buying position on the local stock market also helped the won recover most of its early losses, which some dealers attributed to dollar buying by government-run banks early in the morning.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The won closed trading at 1,171.5 to the dollar, down from Friday's 1,170.6, but well above a session low of 1,174.5. It opened at 1,173.0 and touched a high of 1,171.4.  \par \par \uc2 "The government seems to be trying to spread sentiment that the won could gain further after the holidays, but there are not many people who expect that to happen unless the yen moves up steadily and strongly," said a local bank dealer.  \par \par \uc2 South Korean markets will be closed from Wednesday to Friday for a Thanksgiving holiday.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it is opposed to any direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate should be decided by the market's forces. However, it has pledged to conduct "smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 " if the market shows abrupt moves.  \par \par \uc2 On Monday, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said in Basel, Switzerland, that the bank was prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out any abrupt moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 "We let exchange rates be determined by the market itself, but when abrupt change of exchange rates (occurs), we \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out (markets)," Bank of Korea Assistant Governor Lee Jae-ouk told reporters in Basel.  \par \par \uc2 South Korea is keen to stop the won from gaining too much against the dollar, as a firmer won would mean weakening the price edge for its export industries, which severely compete with the Japanese.  \par \par \uc2 That makes the won's rate versus the yen an important barometer. The cross rate, a calculated rate based on each \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value against the dollar, stood at 10.0162/22 late on Monday versus 10.0111 late on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said dollar offers from exporters were steady ahead of the September 10-12 Chusok holidays as many companies paid special bonuses to employees ahead of and during the country's major traditional festival.  \par \par \uc2 In Tokyo, the yen slid against the dollar on Monday amid persistent talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities to halt a recent rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The yen fell towards 117.75 to the dollar from 116.80 late in New York trade on Friday and 3-1/2-month highs at around 115.75 set last week.  \par \par \uc2 On the non-deliverable forward market, one-month won was quoted at 1,173.7/4.7 late on Monday versus 1,172.8/3.8 late on Friday and three-month won at 1,179.0/80.0 versus 1,178.0/9.0.  \par \par \uc2 Seoul's main bourse closed down 0.17 percent but foreign investors, who were net sellers of local shares in early morning, bought a net 143.3 billion won ($122.5 million) worth of shares by the end of the day. ($1=1170.0 Won).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030908dz98001c2