\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-China digs in heels over  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  system - S.Korea. \b0\par\par\uc2 440 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2003\par 14:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MANILA, Aug 7 (Reuters) - China has told fellow members of the ASEAN Plus Three grouping that it does not intend to loosen its grip for now on the yuan, South Korean Deputy Finance Minister Tae-Shin Kwon said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "They have no plan to change their exchange rate system for the time being," Kwon told reporters at a gathering of finance ministers and their deputies from the Association of South East Asian Nations (ASEAN) plus China, South Korea and Japan. \par \par \uc2  "Our Chinese colleague is still very stubborn," he added. \par \par \uc2  China, whose delegation is led by Finance Minister Jin Renqing, explained that Beijing was taking steps to relieve upward pressure on the yuan , or renminbi, by allowing more capital outflows, Kwon said. \par \par \uc2  "But it cannot be a fundamental solution," he commented. \par \par \uc2  Kwon said the representative at the meeting of the People's Bank of China, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had been particularly firm in his insistence that the policy would remain on hold. \par \par \uc2  Kwon said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets should be in line with International Monetary Fund rules, which permit smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  but say governments may not artificially manipulate their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "If a certain country tries to target a certain level of exchange rate through heavy and frequent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that may be against IMF guidelines," Kwon said. \par \par \uc2  Kwon was speaking a day after Japan reported that it spent more than 4.6 trillion yen between April and June, equivalent to $38.36 billion, to hold down the yen. \par \par \uc2  Japan has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in record amounts this year to prevent a rise in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from hurting the country's exporters. \par \par \uc2  Kwon said that whenever \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it does so in line with IMF guidelines. \par \par \uc2  The authorities needed to step in occasionally because the South Korean interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market had daily turnover of less than $3 billion, resulting at times in either a dearth or an excess of dollars, Kwon said. \par \par \uc2  He noted that whereas the yen's real exchange rate had risen by 5.4 percent since January 2002, the won had appreciated by 12.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  "That is evidence that South Korea is letting the market decide the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate," Kwon said. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether the South Korean won's cross rate of around 9.87 to the yen meant the yen was too weak, Kwon said: "I think so." \par \par \uc2  Japanese and South Korean exporters are keen competitors across a range of industries, and market economists believe Seoul is more comfortable when the cross rate is above, rather than below, 10 won per yen. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030807dz87007q2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
