\par\par \b \uc2  S.Korean won opens at 5-mth low on looming Iraq war. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Song Jung-a and Lee Soo-jung \par \uc2 573 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 03:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, March 20 (Reuters) - The South Korean won  opened at a five-month low on Thursday, as importers chased dollars, wary about uncertainties stemming from a near certain war in Iraq and its fallout on the fragile economy. \par \par \uc2  The won gapped down to 1,264.0 per dollar at the opening, falling for a fourth consecutive session. It closed at 1,256.8 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar buying by importers continued as a U.S.-led military strike against Iraq looks imminent after a U.S. deadline for Iraqi President Saddam Hussein to leave the country or face war passed with no sign of compliance from the Iraqi President. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese yen  also fell to a one-month low of 120.65 in early trade, as investors chased the dollar amid expectations of a swift and decisive war. \par \par \uc2  WARY OVER INTERNVENTION \par \par \uc2  Worried about the sliding won, South Korea's finance ministry issued a fresh warning on Thursday, saying it would take steps to stabilise the market, if needed. \par \par \uc2  "We're worried about excessively weak sentiment on the won (against the dollar)," a high-ranking ministry official told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  After the warning, the won cut some of its losses, trading at 1,261.9 per dollar at 0110 GMT. But dealers expected the won's downward trend would continue throughout the day. \par \par \uc2  They forecast the won would move between 1,257 won and 1,267 won, keeping an eye on whether the won would breach the previous low of 1,267.50 touched in mid-October. \par \par \uc2  "Korean companies are hoarding dollars ahead of the war," a foreign bank dealer said. "The downward trend will continue, but investors need to be cautious, as authorities may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's state-run banks were seen selling dollars on Wednesday, after a series of warnings by government officials of the flagging won. \par \par \uc2  Traders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market since last week, selling dollars to support the won. They said the Bank of Korea had spent about $1 billion to support the Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  An official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to confirm the reports. But South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell to $123.54 billion by March 15, compared with $123.99 billion at the end of February, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  ANOTHER CRISIS NOT LIKELY \par \par \uc2  Sentiment was also hurt after a local newspaper said hedge funds had been attacking the won, Asia's worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, in offshore markets in early March. \par \par \uc2  Traders said some speculative forces were spotted, but could not say which funds were buying what quantity of dollars. \par \par \uc2  "Investors are selling the won, due to the slowing economy and geopolitical risks involving Iraq and North Korea," a local bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  North Korea is currently locked in a standoff with the United States over its suspected nuclear weapons programme. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund recently said the country's economic growth could slow to three percent this year under the worst case scenario, around half of last year's estimated growth. \par \par \uc2  "But the situation is not as serious as in late 1997, when the Asian financial crisis broke out," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  South Korea, Asia's fourth-largest economy, teetered on the brink of national default in late 1997, as the won was severely attacked by foreign speculators. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030320dz3k003v4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
