\par\par \b \uc2  S.Korea c.bank may  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  against  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  speculation. \b0\par\par\uc2 130 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 01:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, March 19 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday it could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if it considers the won  is being depressed by speculative trading. \par \par \uc2  "We can \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market through verbal warning or other means, if the won gets weaker due to speculators," Lee Jae-wook, deputy governor at the Bank of Korea, told economic researchers. \par \par \uc2  He also said authorities would prevent the weaker won from adversely affecting the fragile economy. \par \par \uc2  The Korean won opened lower at 1,250.0 against dollar on Wednesday, after it slid to a fresh five-month closing low on Tuesday, hit by concerns that an imminent U.S.-led military strike against Iraq may damage the economy. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030319dz3j001jq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
