\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Fall;Won At 5-Mo Low,Peso At New Trough \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nirmala Menon \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 744 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 10:06\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Korean won ended at a five month trough and the Philippine peso dived to yet another historic closing low as most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened in tandem with the yen Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  North Korea tensions and accounting scandals at SK Global weighed on the won while domestic security concerns and fears of a war in Iraq again took a toll on the peso. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader in Singapore said most central banks in the region were in the markets, "just to smoothen things out, nothing too major." \par \par \uc2  He suspected that some U.S. investment houses were selling part of their investments in the region because of renewed security concerns in the Philippines, and the North Korea tensions. \par \par \uc2  The won gave up all the previous day's gains and ended at the intraday low of KRW1,245.0 to the dollar, from KRW1,229.9 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  A last flurry of dollar buying sent the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soaring about seven won in the final minutes of the session as market players dared to test higher and higher levels in the absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Very vague and unconfirmed rumors that North and \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchanged gunfire Wednesday supported the dollar and underpinned the uneasy and cautious feelings of many market participants. However, there were no reputable media reports of any such incident. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think it (the rumor) is true. If that rumor were real, it would be a big emergency," said a dealer. "Many people are nervous and are making things up." \par \par \uc2  Chong Hui Chin, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan Chase Bank, said some people believed that beyond Iraq, "North Korea could be a big wildcard in the geopolitical perspective." \par \par \uc2  But she said the bank still believes the issue will be resolved diplomatically though "in the meantime, we don't discount more provocations from North Korea." That should put a floor under the dollar/won, she added. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, foreign selling of stocks in the last two days over the SK Global woes also translated into dollar buying in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Prosecutors Tuesday said they uncovered KRW1.55 trillion of accounting irregularities at the trading company. \par \par \uc2  Rising military tension on the Korean peninsula also weighed on the New Taiwan dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit ended at NT$34.721, up from NT$34.620 Tuesday. The intraday range was between NT$34.639 and NT$34.727. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso extended losses to finish at a new all time closing low as companies hedged dollar requirements and banks built up their holdings of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The peso ended at 55.099 to the dollar on the Philippine Dealing System, wiping out the previous record closing low of PHP54.900 reached on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market is focused on a March 17 deadline set by the U.S. for Iraq to disarm, or face war. \par \par \uc2  Locally, traders are bracing for more violence as latest reports from southern Philippines said authorities detonated explosive devices in Davao, the site of last week's deadly bombing. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar slipped to a three-week low Wednesday as local banks covered short U.S. dollar positions as the U.S. unit rose against the yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The aggressive buying again fueled speculation that local banks were snapping up U.S. dollars on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore, although most dealers said that was unlikely. \par \par \uc2  "Across the board U.S. dollar short covering has been influential today, but Singapore dollar has also depreciated of its own accord this week," Bank of America said in a research note. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of local trading hours, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7463, up from S$1.7362 late Tuesday, but slightly off the intraday high of S$1.7467, which was its strongest level in three weeks. \par \par \uc2  The intraday range of S$1.7380-S$1.7467 was the widest so far this month. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht weakened in line with its regional counterparts and was quoted at THB42.68 to the dollar late in Asia, from THB42.46 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah was quoted at 8,892 to the dollar, compared with IDR8,885 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Barry Deely \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030312dz3c000kl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
