\par\par \b \uc2  S Korea Won Ends At New 6-Mo Closing High KRW1,173.2/Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 680 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2003\par 08:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL -(Dow Jones)- The South Korean won ended at a new six-month high Thursday on expectations of equity-linked dollar inflows and on bets of more downside risk in the U.S \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the yen, said participants. \par \par \uc2  "It is difficult to have long dollar-won positions because people think the dollar-yen will go down again," said a dealer. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 1,173.2 won, below Wednesday's finish at KRW1,175.4, its worst close since ending at KRW1,170.9 July 25. The dollar kept to a tight band between KRW1,172.2 and KRW1,176.0 during the session. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened flat, but then offshore players sold dollars, pressuring the dollar down to the KRW1,172-level and prompting long players to sell as well, participants said. \par \par \uc2  Trade was lackluster throughout the session due to concerns over possible government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  both in \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japan. The nervousness is helping to keep the dollar supported at KRW1,170. \par \par \uc2  But some say the key support level could be tested Friday due to expectations of continued dollar weakness against the yen, which would pressure the dollar against the won as well. \par \par \uc2  "The yen is getting stronger daily, inch by inch, and will go to 116 in the first quarter," said a dealer. \par \par \uc2  Also, local stock market-related inflows are expected Friday as foreign investors were net buyers of about KRW9 billion in shares Thursday and KRW123.3 billion Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is expected to trade between 1,168-1,175 Friday. \par \par \uc2  At 0805 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y118.01, below Y118.10 in New York and Y118.15 in Tokyo late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The yen was at KRW9.94 late Thursday, just below KRW9.96 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  South Korean bond yields slid to new closing lows Thursday but came off morning lows after a government official reportedly said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  feels the recent decline in bond yields wouldn't last for long, market participants said. \par \par \uc2  The yield on benchmark three-year treasurys fell two basis points to 4.96%, a new 14-month low. The yield on five-year treasurys fell two basis points to 5.14%, a fresh 15-month low. \par \par \uc2  Bond gains came due to the still-favorable supply-demand situation. \par \par \uc2  About an hour into trading, an unnamed government official said, "The latest (yield) fall doesn't seem to reflect the (economic) fundamentals well enough," according to Yonhap Infomax. \par \par \uc2  The official also reportedly said the Bank of Korea would carefully consider the size of its weekly monetary stabilization bond auctions. Traders took that to mean the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could overwhelm the market with supply to at least slow the decline of yields. \par \par \uc2  "The comments were very aggressive and intentional," said a trader. "There will be a two-to-three basis points upward correction (of yields). But, there are not much bonds to sell." \par \par \uc2  Indeed, due to the overall lack of supply - the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy has no plans to offer bonds again until February - market sentiment remains positive, said participants. \par \par \uc2  Partly due to the favorable supply-demand dynamics, JPMorgan said in its monthly report the yield on three-year government bonds may test 4.80% or lower in the near term. A delay in monetary tightening and geopolitical concerns will also support a rise in bond prices, JPMorgan said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                                Thu      Wed\par 3-Yr Treasury:                4.96% vs 4.98%\par 5-Yr Treasury:                5.14% vs 5.16%\par 10-Yr Treasury:               5.42% vs 5.43%\par 1-Yr Stabilization Bond:      4.75% vs 4.75%\par 2-Yr Stabilization Bond:      4.89% vs 4.90%\par 3-Yr Corporate AA- Bonds:     5.45% vs 5.47%\par 3-Yr Corporate BBB- Bonds:    8.82% vs 8.84%\par 3-Mo Certificates of Deposit: 4.66% vs 4.74%\par One-Day Call Rate:            4.24% vs 4.28%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Kim Junghwa and Laura Elizabeth Pohl, Dow Jones Newswires;\par 822-732-2165, djn.seoul@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1063 1858 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030116dz1g000f6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
