\par\par \b \uc2  S Korea Won Ends Weaker As Yen Falls, Importers Buy Dlrs \b0\par\par\uc2 678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Januar 2003\par 09:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL -(Dow Jones)- The South Korean won weakened Thursday as the yen lost ground and importers bought dollars, with dollar short covering adding to the won's losses, market players said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  finished at 1,192.9 above Tuesday's close at KRW1,186.5. The dollar changed hands between KRW1,188.0 and KRW1,193.4. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets were closed Wednesday for the New Year holiday. \par \par \uc2  Trade was relatively light Thursday since players are still on holiday; the thin trading conditions exaggerated the won's moves. \par \par \uc2  "The market was thin and so the market was volatile. When the dollar hit KRW1,188 there were importers buying," said a dealer. He added that market players aren't very certain of the won's direction since the Japanese financial markets are closed until Monday. \par \par \uc2  Concern that the Bank of Japan could actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market there to push up the exchange rate when markets open again also pressured the won, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The won often tracks the yen due to the similar export portfolios of \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japan. \par \par \uc2  "There are jitters about BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  At 0755 GMT, the dollar was at Y119.39, up from Y118.70 in New York late Tuesday and Y118.51 in Asia Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The yen was at KRW10.0, up from KRW9.99 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is likely to trade between KRW1,190 and KRW1,200 on Friday, market participants said. \par \par \uc2  South Korean bond yields again tumbled to fresh closing lows Thursday due to geopolitical uncertainties, market participants said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy jumped into the market near the session's close to basically approve of the recent fall in yields, but market participants didn't react much. \par \par \uc2  Rates have been falling for the past three weeks or so on North Korea's nuclear posturing and the growing likelihood of a U.S.-led war in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  "Their (MOFE's) comment is right, but everyone already knew it," said a trader. \par \par \uc2  An unnamed government official said the recent drop in key South Korea bond yields is natural and inevitable given the stock market's losses lately, according to Yonhap Infomax. The official also said the yield on the benchmark three-year treasury could breach 5%, an opinion some market participants held even before the official's remarks. \par \par \uc2  However, Kim Bum-Joong, an analyst at Daewoo Securities, said the benchmark yield isn't likely to drop under 5.00%. He pointed out the yield on one-year monetary stabilization bonds is 4.98% with not much room to drop further since the Bank of Korea must redeem a large amount of monetary stabilization bonds in January. \par \par \uc2  "It (the redemptions) will prevent the short-end rate from going down," he said. \par \par \uc2  The yield on benchmark three-year government bonds slid four basis points to a 14-month closing low of 5.07%. The yield on five-year government bonds fell five basis points to a 15-month closing low of 5.29%. \par \par \uc2  A tighter supply of bonds for the month of January also helped bond prices on Thursday. The government will auction only KRW820 billion worth of three-year treasurys on Jan. 6, with no supply for the rest of the month. \par \par \uc2  Kim expects the three-year government bond to trade between 5.00% and 5.40% for the rest of January. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                                Thu      Tue\par 3-Yr Treasury:                5.07% vs 5.11%\par 5-Yr Treasury:                5.29% vs 5.34%\par 10-Yr Treasury:               5.57% vs 5.61%\par 1-Yr Stabilization Bond:      4.98% vs 4.99%\par 2-Yr Stabilization Bond:      5.08% vs 5.12%\par 3-Yr Corporate AA- Bonds:     5.64% vs 5.68%\par 3-Yr Corporate BBB- Bonds:    9.03% vs 9.08%\par 3-Mo Certificates of Deposit: 4.90% vs 4.90%\par One-Day Call Rate:            4.27% vs 4.34%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Laura Elizabeth Pohl, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165,\par laura.pohl@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1063 1858 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030102dz12000cs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
