\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Up Late As Yen Gains Ground \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nirmala Menon \par \uc2 870 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 2002\par 12:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  shrugged off early losses to trade mostly higher late Thursday, taking the cue from the yen's recovery. \par \par \uc2  Several local developments, notably an upgrade of Indonesia's sovereign rating, were ignored by \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  participants who preferred to look to the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for direction amid jitters over possible U.S. military action against Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that although sentiment on Indonesia was helped by Standard & Poor's upgrade of the country's sovereign rating to CCC+ from selective default, the rupiah scarcely moved. \par \par \uc2  The upgrade was largely expected as S&P had flagged the move for months, but its rating is still one rung below Moody's Investor Service, which has a B3 rating and positive outlook. Furthermore, Indonesia's rating remains well below investment grade, reflecting the country's inability to repay debt it has borrowed from overseas. Earlier this year, S&P downgraded Indonesia's long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating to selective default from CCC previously. \par \par \uc2  In late Asian hours, the dollar was trading at 8,865 rupiah, unchanged from Wednesday's close, but below the day's high at IDR8,884 reached in the morning. Traders said the rupiah's intraday gains were helped by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  which sold dollars near the intraday high. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help the rupiah but doesn't confirm specific actions. \par \par \uc2  However, the dollar's downside was protected at IDR8,550 by local companies with foreign debt, as well as importers. The dollar should trade between IDR8,550 and IDR8,900 Friday, traders said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Philippines' August Inflation Above Expectations\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the Philippines, stronger-than-expected inflation data for August\par foreshadowed a rising price trend, but the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said monetary\par policy is likely to stay neutral for the rest of the year, suggesting\par interest rates won't be hiked.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Philippine's consumer price index rose 2.9% on year in August, up from\par 2.6% in July, and higher than the 2.72% average in a Dow Jones Newswires\par poll of private economists. The figure also exceeded the government's\par expectations.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor Rafael Buenaventura said "it should have no\par immediate impact (on policy rates) given that inflation is still way\par below target."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "But it reinforces our neutral stance taken since the early part of\par this year," he added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders said the data weren't worrisome, with markets largely\par shrugging off the numbers. The dollar closed at 51.72 pesos on the\par Philippine Dealing System, down from PHP51.845 Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "What will have more of an impact on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market is the\par movement in the yen," a bank dealer in Manila said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts agreed, noting that Asian units will likely to continue\par taking the cue from the major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But heightened risk aversion ahead of the first anniversary of the\par Sept. 11 terrorist attacks in the U.S., and prospects of the U.S.\par launching a war against Iraq kept market participants jittery.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Risk appetite globally is not building, which is not bringing\par stimulus for a rally (in Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 )," said Chong Hui Chin,\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at J.P. Morgan Chase Bank in Singapore.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Concerns about the U.S. economic health are also never far away from\par the minds of market participants, with data so far sending mixed\par signals, traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par To that end, markets will be keeping close watch on more economic\par indicators due from the U.S later Thursday and Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ended at a two-week low against the New Taiwan\par dollar as exporters sold the former, especially as key levels were\par triggered, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar closed at NT$34.122, down from NT$34.236 Wednesday, after\par moving between NT$34.105 and NT$34.266 intraday. It was the weakest\par close for the dollar since Aug. 22.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said market participants sold dollars once it broke through\par NT$34.20, a stop-loss level. But some short-covering activities helped\par offset the dollar's losses.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was reportedly in the market to curb the local unit's\par rise, but it didn't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aggressively or too visibly, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , dollar sales linked to the non-deliverable forwards\par market, coupled with the yen's firmer tone, weighed down the U.S.\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar ended at 1,191.0 won, down from KRW1,195.3 at the previous\par day's close, after trading between KRW1,190.0 and KRW1,198.0 intraday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The market is basically watching dollar/yen, but again, lots of\par players don't want to escape from the KRW1,190 to KRW1,210 range," said\par Oh Suktae, a Citibank economist.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also slipped against the Singapore dollar and the\par Thai baht, both of which tracked the yen higher.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As of 1022 GMT, the dollar was quoted at S$1.7438, down from S$1.7455\par Wednesday. Against the baht, the dollar was quoted at THB42.13, from\par THB42.18 previously.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046;\par nirmala.menon@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020905dy95000nb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
