\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late;  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Steadies US Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 767 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2002\par 11:51\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The struggling U.S. dollar stabilized against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday with the help of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by monetary authorities in \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Taiwan. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar finished Asian trading hours little changed against the won, New Taiwan dollar, Singapore dollar and Thai baht, after falling to multimonth lows against all four a day earlier. The Indonesian rupiah and Philippine peso weakened. \par \par \uc2  However, any strengthening of the U.S. dollar is likely to be short lived because the underlying sentiment in global markets continues to be biased heavily against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. dollar buying by state-run Korean banks saved the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from posting a new low against the won. The state run banks swung into action after the U.S. dollar fell to around 1,172 won early in the session after closing a 19-month low of KRW1,179.4 a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they believed state-run banks bought around $500 million on behalf of the government. The move helped the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebound to close at KRW1,179.5. \par \par \uc2  A senior Bank of Korea official later hinted at the government's involvement in the round of U.S. dollar buying, saying it is "natural" for the government to take actions to stabilize exchange rates "when market participants lose their balance." \par \par \uc2  Governments in Korea, Taiwan and Japan have all \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  either directly or indirectly in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets recently to slow the rise of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Authorities have expressed concern that rapid appreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could undermine the price competitiveness of their exporters in global markets. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar tracked its north Asia counterparts to remain steady against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7502, compared with S$1.7505 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  An announcement by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore that it will maintain its neutral policy on the Singapore dollar on a trade-weighted basis for the balance of 2002 had little immediate impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  However, over the longer term it means there will be room for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue appreciating against the U.S. dollar, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  JP Morgan is targeting the U.S. dollar to fall to S$1.72 by the end of September and S$1.70 by the end of December. \par \par \uc2  Investors favor countries with positive cash flows like Singapore during times of stock market volatility and heightened risk aversion, said Thio Chin Loo, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's large current account surplus, healthy foreign reserves, and balanced budget are all supportive of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because they make Singapore less vulnerable to volatility in global capital flows, she said. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at NT$33.284 compared with NT$33.282 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to smooth out volatility, bought around US$700 million throughout the day, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar would have substantially eclipsed its 14-month high set Wednesday if not for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's actions to prop up the U.S. dollar, one trader said. \par \par \uc2  Against the rupiah the U.S dollar broke through the key IDR9,000 resistance level for the first time in six sessions. \par \par \uc2  A 1.4% decline in the main index of the Jakarta Stock Exchange and U.S. dollar short covering weighed on the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  At the end of Jakarta trading the U.S. dollar was quoted at a two-month high of IDR9,125, up from IDR8,915 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was rumored to have sold the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to keep the rupiah below 9,000 per U.S. dollar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar traded in a narrow range against the baht, finishing the session at THB41.160, compared with a 22-month low of THB41.150 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Thailand's Commerce Minister Adisai Bodharamik said the recent strength in the baht against the U.S. dollar hasn't harmed export growth, which is expected to be sustained in June. \par \par \uc2  Adisai told reporters that the country's exports will remain competitive as the baht strengthens along with other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region against the weak U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  He said exports were estimated to rise by between 3.5% and 4.2% in June from a year ago. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4045; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par