\par\par \b \uc2  S Korea Fin Min: Estimates 2Q GDP Growth At 6%-7% \b0\par\par\uc2 677 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2002\par 01:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL -(Dow Jones)- The South Korean economy will likely grow 6%-7% on year in the second quarter, Minister of Finance and Economy Jeon Yun-churl said Friday, citing forecasts from state-run research institutes. \par \par \uc2  Those institutes expect about a 7% on year growth for the entire 2002, Jeon said. \par \par \uc2  The government firmly maintains its official gross domestic product forecast between 5% and 5.9% in 2002. \par \par \uc2  The government is expected to update the forecast around  the end of this month when it reviews the economy's blueprint for the second half. \par \par \uc2  Finance minister Jeon said he doesn't expect major changes in \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's "macroeconomic policy framework" in the second half. \par \par \uc2  Government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, or any other markets, "isn't a good policy," he said. \par \par \uc2  The government has repeatedly issued verbal comments recently to support the dollar, which has depreciated sharply against the won since mid-April. But it has reiterated that any direct dollar purchases will only be undertaken when, and if the market becomes extremely volatile. \par \par \uc2  A rapid and sharp rise in the won can weigh on the country's export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  Regarding Seoulbank (Q.SBK), Jeon said 15 unidentified investors are interested in buying a controlling stake in the bank, which is wholly owned by the government. \par \par \uc2  The government hopes to select a buyer for Seoulbank by end-July, he said. \par \par \uc2  The government is trying to sell a controlling stake in Seoulbank or a merger with another bank as part of its privatization efforts. \par \par \uc2  South Korea's medium-sized business group Dongwon Group (Q.DWG) and a consortium led by Dongbu Group have said they will bid for Seoulbank. \par \par \uc2  Chohung Bank (Q.CHB), Korea Exchange Bank (Q.KEB) and Hana Bank (Q.HMB) have also shown an interest in merging with Seoulbank. \par \par \uc2  Jeon said he "personally" think the government needs to raise price of Korea Life Insurance Co., another company owned by the government. \par \par \uc2  The government has been in talks with Hanwha Group (Q.HWG) to sell a controlling stake in the insurer. \par \par \uc2  But the talks have stalled recently because the government wants to raise the pricetag on Korea Life, citing improvement in its business performance. \par \par \uc2  Jeon also said South Korean authorities will need more time to decide on any possible call rate hike. His remarks were in line with what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Friday morning, that it needs more time to observe economic conditions to determine interest rate policy. \par \par \uc2  While the global demand recovery isn't strong just yet, the won's appreciation against the dollar and stable crude oil prices are helping ease inflation concerns, he said. \par \par \uc2  The effect of increasing exports and capital investment, which will accompany the ongoing economic recovery, on inflation will be "neutral," he said, without elaborating. \par \par \uc2  The current weakness in the local stock market is due to the influence of falling U.S. equities recently, Jeon said. "Again, it's not desirable to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  deeply in such a trend in the market." \par \par \uc2  With reference to the restructuring of Hynix Semiconductor Inc. (Q.HYY), Jeon said "market principle" will dictate the resolution of the chipmaker's problems. \par \par \uc2  He downplayed nationalistic concerns that selling Hynix to a foreign investor will hurt South Korea's economy, thereby leaving room for further talks with foreign partners. He added that the government wants "a clear resolution," but again he didn't elaborate on this. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Jeon said financial companies that have benefitted from previous public fund injections will mostly have to shoulder the burden of any losses with the government. "The responsibility goes where the profit arises," he said. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean government has spent a total of 156 trillion won ($1=KRW1,221.5) in recapitalizing ailing financial companies since the Asian financial crisis in 1997. It won't be able to recover some of the money spent to pay deposits at failed firms. \par \par \uc2  -By So-Eui Rhee and Shin Jung-won, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165; djn.seoul@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020620dy6k001c3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
