\par\par \b \uc2  S Korea Won Ends Lower On Govt Verbal  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 831 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juni 2002\par 10:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL -(Dow Jones)- The South Korean won finished weaker against the dollar Friday on repeated verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk by the government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials, and dollar buying from state companies, said participants. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 1,227.2 won, higher than Wednesday's close of KRW1,224.3. \par \par \uc2  South Korean markets were closed Thursday for a national holiday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which opened at KRW1,226.5, traded in a range of KRW1,225.5 to KRW1,230.9 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar/won swayed throughout the day amid verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the dollar from falling further after its recent spell of multi-month lows, although dollar selling by exporters capped any substantial rise for the U.S. unit. \par \par \uc2  The government's plan to increase the planned issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilization bonds to KRW700 billion in June from KRW500 billion originally also fueled speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, participants said. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk - once in the morning and twice in the afternoon - together with dollar buying from state-run Korea Electric Power Corp. and Korea Development Bank pushed the dollar above KRW1,230. But the dollar then fell to KRW1,220 on heavy selling by exporters, such as electronics and shipbuilding companies, they said. \par \par \uc2  A trader at Hana Bank said state companies bought around $100 million Friday, while local exporters sold $150 million. They sold dollars above the KRW1,228 level. \par \par \uc2  Verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the government in support of the dollar, which first began late last month, intensified this week as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell briefly below the key KRW1,220 level. The dollar reached an 18-month intraday low of KRW1,218.7 Wednesday on three rounds of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders speculate that state companies, which have persistently been buying dollars this week, were likely acting on behalf of the government. \par \par \uc2  "The government still seems to be uncomfortable with the dollar/won level," said a trader at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  He said the government hopes the dollar will rise to around KRW1,230, a level that will likely keep the country's exports competitive. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Korea Governor Park Seung said earlier Friday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may buy dollars in the market if the dollar/won weakens further, as such a scenario would heighten concerns over what impact a stronger local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have on the country's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy said early in the afternoon that the speed and scale of the strengthening won against the dollar is too excessive compared with gains in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the country's major competitors. \par \par \uc2  In a statement, the ministry said the government will take whatever steps are necessary to keep the country's economic recovery on track, without actually saying it would act in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  then reiterated later that it's closely monitoring the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and is concerned about the uncertainties prevailing among market players. \par \par \uc2  They expect the dollar to trade in a range of KRW1,220 to KRW1,235 next week on expectations of more government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , verbal or actual. \par \par \uc2  Prices of \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's key benchmark bonds rose Friday due to a weak local stock market and a rally in U.S. Treasurys overnight, market participants said. \par \par \uc2  South Korean stocks ended lower Friday, tracking a poor performance on U.S. markets, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The Korea Composite Stock Price Index, or Kospi, fell 14.43 points, or 1.8%, to 795.16 Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Continuing weakness in stock markets brings some concerns over the speed of the economic recovery, which is helping improve bond buying sentiment," says Daewoo Securities analyst Kim Bum-Joon. \par \par \uc2  Also, some imbalance of supply and demand due to limited market liquidity caused yields to fall, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Korea canceled window sales of monetary stabilization bonds Friday due to lack of demand, market participants said. \par \par \uc2  Key Treasury bond yields are approaching the support level of 6% but are likely to move higher next week after the triple-witching-day in the local stock market Wednesday, said Yang Jin-Mo, an analyst at SK Securities Co. \par \par \uc2  Triple witching occurs when the contracts for stock index futures, stock index options and individual stock options all expire on the same day. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                                 Fri       Wed\par 3-Yr Treasury:                 6.05% vs  6.10%\par 5-Yr Treasury:                 6.45% vs  6.50%\par 10-Yr Treasury:                6.86% vs  6.89%\par 1-Yr Stabilization Bond:       5.36% vs  5.37%\par 2-Yr Stabilization Bond:       5.98% vs  6.01%\par 3-Yr Corporate AA- Bonds:      6.83% vs  6.86%\par 3-Yr Corporate BBB- Bonds:    10.77% vs 10.81%\par 3-Mo Certificates of Deposit:  4.83% vs  4.83%\par One-Day Call Rate:             4.17% vs  4.33%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Shin Jung-won and Nara Han, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165;\par jung-won.shin@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1063 1858 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020607dy67000hy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
