\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Up Late; Reach Multi-month Highs Vs Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2 753 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2002\par 12:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  surged against the dollar Friday - some to their highest levels in more than a year - as improving prospects for economic recovery continued to stir investors' interest in the region. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won and Thai baht strengthened to 15-month highs, while the Philippine peso posted its best performance against the dollar in 14 months. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah hit an eight-month high, the Singapore dollar a seven-month high and the New Taiwan dollar a five-month high. \par \par \uc2  "We're starting to see good growth and export numbers from Asia. Current account surpluses are rising and countries are attracting portfolio inflows as well as foreign direct investment," said Thio Chin Loo, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Greater tolerance for risk has whetted investor's appetites for emerging market assets, particularly those in Asia, she said. \par \par \uc2  The yen's strength also set a firmer tone for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Authorities in \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  attempted to curb the won's rise with verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy issued a statement saying it was concerned about the dollar's sharp fall against the won and would take action if warranted. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose slightly after the statement, but resumed its slide when the ministry didn't act on its warning. \par \par \uc2  The limited impact from the government's statement was also due in part to seemingly contradictory remarks from Finance Minister Jeon Yun-churl, made at almost the same time. \par \par \uc2  Jeon said that dollar/won exchange rate reflects the health of South Korea's economy, which traders interpreted to mean the government was comfortable with the won's strength. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at KRW1,261.6, down from Thursday's close at KRW1,269.8. Friday's close marked the won's strongest finish against the dollar since March 1, 2001. \par \par \uc2  Rising regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and recent upbeat comments from the government regarding Thailand's export prospects underpinned the baht. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading, the dollar was quoted at THB42.740, down from THB42.845 late Thursday. It was the baht's best close since Feb. 20, 2001. \par \par \uc2  The peso got swept up in the euphoria driving other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at 49.46 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, down from PHP49.52 Thursday. It was the peso's strongest finish since March 29, 2001. \par \par \uc2  The peso also was supported by remittance inflows from overseas Filipino workers and thin corporate demand for the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  There was talk in the market that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took advantage of the cheap U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy about US$30 million to boost its reserves, which helped contain the peso's rise. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah was buoyed by gains in its regional peers and continued optimism over the government's privatization program. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the U.S. dollar was quoted at 9,080 rupiah, down from IDR9,150 late Thursday. It was the rupiah's best showing since Sept. 14, 2001. \par \par \uc2  Still, the rupiah was unable to seriously test key resistance at IDR9,000 to the U.S. dollar as local companies that owe debt overseas picked up the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the lower levels. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was helped by strong economic data and comments from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore that it's comfortable with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current level against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7950, down from S$1.8024 late Thursday. It was the Singapore dollar's strongest performance since Oct. 5, 2001. \par \par \uc2  The government reported that non-oil domestic exports rose a surprising 6.4% on year, the first increase after 13 consecutive months of decline. \par \par \uc2  MAS Assistant Managing Director Khor Hoe Ee told reporters that the trade-weighted Singapore dollar is still within the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's undisclosed policy band, "and we are comfortable with it." \par \par \uc2  Foreign funds flowing into Taiwan from the recent sale of US$700 million worth of convertible bonds by Cathay Financial Holding helped lift the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.458, down from NT$34.538 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  It was its strongest close for the New Taiwan dollar since Dec. 12, 2001. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020517dy5h000uc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
