\par\par \b \uc2  Tsy  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Rpt Says Dollar Strength Reflects Strong US Econ \b0\par\par\uc2 532 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2002\par 02:42\par \uc2  Dow Jones News Service \par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON -(Dow Jones)- The strength of the U.S. dollar last year was largely the result of stronger U.S. economic fundamentals compared with major trading partners, rather than deliberate attempts by trading partners to devalue their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to a U.S. Treasury Department report on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy obtained by Dow  Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  No major U.S. trading partners were guilty of manipulating their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gain a trade advantage during the second half of 2001, Treasury said in its Report to Congress on International Economic and Exchange Rate Policies. The report, mandated by U.S. trade laws, outlines economic and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market developments around the world, and is due to be presented at a hearing before the Senate Banking Committee Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The release of the report comes at a time when U.S. manufacturers are complaining that the continued strength of  the U.S. dollar is making it impossible for them to compete  abroad.  They blame market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly in Asia, and the Treasury's oft-repeated statements extolling the economic benefits of a strong dollar for their troubles. \par \par \uc2  In its report, Treasury noted the euro managed to break $0.90 only during a two-month period between mid-August to mid-October. \par \par \uc2  "The euro's failure to sustain an appreciation against the dollar during 2001 was, at least partially attributable to Europe's vulnerability to the worldwide slowdown," Treasury said. \par \par \uc2  Japan, whose \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was confined to the period between Sept. 17-28 following the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks, sold about $27 billion worth of yen during that time. During the second half of last year, the yen depreciated 5.8% against the dollar, ending the year at Y131.8. \par \par \uc2  While the Bank of Japan has attempted to fight deflation  by allowing the monetary base to grow sharply during the year, the strategy has shown no signs its working. \par \par \uc2  "The weakness in the growth in broad money is a reflection of the weak balance sheets of Japan's financial and corporate sectors," Treasury said.  "Vigorous action is needed to tackle these twin problems." \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar also weakened against the dollar last year.  Its long-term decline is in part due to the drop  in inflation-adjusted non-energy commodity prices, Treasury  said, citing the Bank of Canada. \par \par \uc2  Most Asian countries eased monetary policy in line with the Federal Reserve last year. However, reminiscent of Japan, those actions didn't translate into stronger credit growth in most countries, "given that many financial institutions remained capital constrained and many corporations remained overly indebted," Treasury said. Given  weak growth and the threat of deflation,  central banks throughout Asia accumulated reserves to limit the appreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par South Korea's increase in official foreign reserves stemmed from\par interest earnings and repayment of loans to South Korean banks extended\par during the Asian crisis, rather than direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, Treasury said. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's gross \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves totaled $102.8 billion at the end of 2001.\par    -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; (202) 862-9295;\par elizabeth.price@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dj00000020020426dy4q0003y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
