\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Down Late; But Off Lows, Tracking Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2 747 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2002\par 10:24\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended the week only slightly lower as the dollar's failure to make a convincing break above Y135 helped them reclaim some ground, observers said. \par \par \uc2  Still, "the yen's decline has probably snapped selected Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of their lethargy," said Thio Chin Loo, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  The dollar briefly poked above the Y135 level earlier Friday, pulling the rug under other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which in recent weeks had been buoyed by portfolio inflows. \par \par \uc2  But those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  regained their footing somewhat along with the yen, which recouped its losses after Japanese financial officials expressed their concern over the speed of the dollar's two-yen rise overnight. \par \par \uc2  At 0900 GMT (4:00 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar was quoted at Y133.98, below Y134.85 in New York late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit a new three-year high of Y135.17 Thursday in New York after U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill said he has no sympathy for U.S. manufacturers' complaints about a strong dollar. Good companies don't "live or die" by exchange rate moves, he argued. Players interpreted his commments to mean that Washington isn't overly concerned about the dollar's strength. \par \par \uc2  Although the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to outperform the yen - given Japan's poor economic prospects - they may well play catch-up soon, especially the Singapore dollar, some analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is most at risk of tracking the yen and the euro lower is the Singapore dollar, which is expensive on a trade-weighted basis," said Thio at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  She added that, with the Singapore stock market vulnerable to profit-taking, the U.S. dollar will likely continue to be supported above S$1.8300. The local stock index is close to the psychologically important 1,800-point threshold. \par \par \uc2  She expects the U.S. dollar to bounce to S$1.8450 in the near term. \par \par \uc2  Late in the day, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.8368, higher than S$1.8320 late Thursday. The yen's earlier slide to Y135 had temporarily dragged the Singapore dollar past S$1.8400 to an intraday low of S$1.8415 against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Expectations of an economic rebound and the specter of a sovereign ratings upgrade for South Korea cushioned the blow on the won from the yen's slide to new three-year lows, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar sales by exporters in South Korea also helped mitigate the pain on the won, dealers added. \par \par \uc2  As a result, the won continued to strengthen against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 1,317.6 won, slightly higher than KRW1,314.4. The won had earlier floundered to as low as KRW1,326.0. \par \par \uc2  The yen was quoted at KRW9.85, after hitting a low of KRW9.74 earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  Haruhiko Kuroda, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, said Friday that there is little Japan can do to control the yen/won cross rate. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda said he "understands" the concerns expressed by Korean officials about the weak yen, given that some Japanese and South Korean exporters directly compete, but added that he can't take steps to control the level of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cross. \par \par \uc2  South Korea - which has in recent months been complaining about the yen's depreciation - appeared resigned to the won's strength against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Kim Yong-duk, deputy minister for international affairs at South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy, said Friday that, although the government is still talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues with Japan, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't considering direct market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - signaling that the government may allow the won to further appreciate against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Exporter U.S. dollar sales helped offset the pressure of the yen on the New Taiwan dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$35.004, slightly higher than NT$34.980 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  On the Philippine Dealing System, the dollar closed at 51.310 pesos, higher than PHP51.235 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at 44.035 baht to the dollar, weaker than THB43.985 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was marginally weaker at 10,325 rupiah against the dollar, compared with IDR10,320 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4043; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020201dy21000k1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
