\par\par \b \uc2  S Korea Won Ends Dn On Yen;Exporters' Dlr Sales Stem Fall \b0\par\par\uc2 725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2002\par 09:20\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL -(Dow Jones)- The South Korean won finished weaker in choppy trade Friday tracking the yen's overnight slide, but exporters' dollar sales helped to cushion the won's fall, said dealers. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 1,317.6 won, up from Thursday's close at KRW1,314.4. The dollar traded between KRW1,315.8 and KRW1,326.0. \par \par \uc2  After a strong start, the dollar lost ground for most of the session as exporters aggressively sold the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and cushioned the won's fall. \par \par \uc2  Remarks from South Korean and Japanese government officials did little to support or boost the dollar, as their comments were either common sense or expected, said traders. \par \par \uc2  Haruhiko Kuroda, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, said Friday there is little Japan can do to control the yen/won cross rate, which hit a low of 9.74 Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The market knows the cross rates are very hard to control," said one trader, when asked why the market didn't react to Kuroda's comments. \par \par \uc2  At about the same time, Kim Yong-duk, deputy minister for international affairs at South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy, said the government is still talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues with the Japanese government. \par \par \uc2  But Kim also said \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not considering direct market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , signaling the government may tolerate a stronger won and a weaker yen. \par \par \uc2  "They (South Korean government officials) don't actually seem to want the weak Korean won at this moment," said Citibank economist Oh Suktae. "The yen/won cross rate could continue to fall." \par \par \uc2  At 0750 GMT (2:50 a.m. EST), the yen was quoted at KRW9.83, above KRW9.74 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at Y134.11, below Y134.85 late Thursday in New York. The dollar was at Y132.78 late Thursday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's two-yen vault to Y135.17 overnight sparked cautionary words from Japan Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa and Zembei Mizoguchi, head of International Bureau. \par \par \uc2  The comments pushed the dollar down sharply, as traders factored in the possibility Japan wants to put the brakes on the yen's decline. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Oh expects the dollar to trade between KRW1,310 and KRW1,330 next\par week.\par - -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par South Korean bonds finished up for a fourth consecutive session Friday\par as the afternoon fall in local share prices added momentum to an early\par liquidity driven rise, said participants.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bonds gained momentum after the Korea Composite Stock Price Index, or\par Kospi, trimmed its earlier gains and finished down 0.8% at 742.42.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market liquidity has been especially ample since the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Economy said Wednesday that it will auction KRW1.3 trillion\par fewer bonds in February than it did in January.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That liquidity helped the market shrug off a potential stumbling block\par early in Friday's session, said participants: data which showed January\par exports fell 8.9% year-on-year compared with the government's\par expectation of a 20% drop.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The data is not good for the bond market," said one trader. "But the\par market still rallies. The market is focused on supply and demand issues.\par There's lots of liquidity."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yield on benchmark three-year government bonds slid eight basis\par points to a four-week low of 5.95%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yield on five-year government bonds fell nine basis points to a\par five-week low of 6.67%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bonds may continue their rally next week, as liquidity should remain\par ample ahead of the Lunar New Year holiday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Neither the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or the Bank of Korea, the country's\par other main bond issuer, is likely to rein in liquidity before the\par holiday, said traders.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                               Fri      Thu\par 3-Yr Treasury:                5.95% vs 6.03%\par 5-Yr Treasury:                6.67% vs 6.76%\par 10-Yr Treasury:               7.08% vs 7.11%\par 1-Yr Stabilization Bond:      4.99% vs 5.06%\par 2-Yr Stabilization Bond:      5.84% vs 5.91%\par 3-Yr Corporate AA- Bonds:     6.90% vs 6.97%\par 3-Yr Corporate BBB Bonds:     11.04% vs 11.12%\par 3-Mo Certificates of Deposit: 4.65% vs 4.67%\par One-Day Call Rate:            3.99% vs 3.98%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Laura Elizabeth Pohl, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165;\par laura.pohl@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1063 1858 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020201dy21000h9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
