\par\par \b \uc2  Korea Vice Fin Min: Rapid Won Gain Vs Dollar Undesirable \b0\par\par\uc2 458 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2001\par 02:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL -(Dow Jones)- The South Korean government will increase its efforts to stabilize the local bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets after the recent sharp gains in the stock market, said Kim Jin-pyo, vice minister of the Finance and Economy Ministry Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The government will adjust the amount of bonds to be issued and their maturities based on market conditions, Kim said. \par \par \uc2  The won's sharp gain against the dollar is "undesirable" and the "government will take appropriate steps to curb the gain if necessary," Kim said. \par \par \uc2  He declined to elaborate on the measures the government may take, however. \par \par \uc2  Kim's comments echo those of a Bank of Korea official in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysis department who said earlier Tuesday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the latest gains against the dollar continue. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's key bond yields have risen sharply on the anticipation that the slowdown in the economy may have hit bottom following the better-than-expected third-quarter gross domestic product data Nov. 22. \par \par \uc2  The won has risen sharply against the dollar recently on the bullish Korean stock market and also on the GDP data. At 0143 GMT (8:43 p.m. EST), the dollar was trading at 1,264 won compared with last Friday's close of KRW1,271.8, but slightly off its high of KRW1,262 hit earlier Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The finance ministry Monday changed the maturity of its KRW500 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilization bond offering to three years from five years. The cut was initiated to minimize the impact of new bond supply on the market, Kim explained. \par \par \uc2  Key economic ministries and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will form a body to work on future changes to the government's bond issue plans, Kim said. \par \par \uc2  The body will review the way the government auctions bonds and look at improving it, he said. The government wants the changes to better reflect market conditions, he added, without elaborating. \par \par \uc2  Kim expects that even after the government-sponsored bond buyout program ends at the end of the year, the beneficiary companies will be able to refinance their maturing bonds without suffering from further liquidity problems. \par \par \uc2  "The government's goal is to eliminate direct support to the markets (as in the bond buyout program) as much as possible," Kim said. \par \par \uc2  Under the bond program, state-run Korea Development Bank (Q.KDV) will have underwritten KRW2.5 trillion in maturing corporate bonds of select troubled companies. Key beneficiaries of the program include Hynix Semiconductor Inc. (Q.HYY), Hyundai Engineering and Construction Co. (Q.HEC) and Hyundai Merchant Marine Co. (Q.HMM) \par \par \uc2  -By So-Eui Rhee, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165; so-eui.rhee@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011127dxbr0002a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
